window.ND=window.ND||{};Object.assign(ND,{"pr|Haemoglobin estimation (Sahli's acid haematin method)":{"d":"## Principle\n- **Decinormal (N/10, 0.1 N) HCl** converts haemoglobin to brown **acid haematin**.\n- The solution is diluted with distilled water until its colour matches the standard brown glass of the comparator.\n- Hb concentration is read directly from the graduated tube in **g/dL** (and in %; 100% = 14.6–14.8 g/dL on many tubes).\n- It is a **visual colorimetric** method.\n\n## Apparatus\n- **Sahli's haemoglobinometer**: comparator box with standard brown glass rods.\n- **Sahli's tube**: graduated in g/dL (2–24) on one side and % on the other.\n- **Haemoglobin pipette**: marked at **20 µL (0.02 mL)**.\n- Stirrer, dropper, N/10 HCl, distilled water.\n- Sterile lancet, spirit swabs, cotton.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Fill Sahli's tube with **N/10 HCl up to the 2 g% (or 10%) mark**.\n2. Under aseptic conditions, make a finger-prick (side of the ring-finger pulp). **Wipe the first drop** and let blood flow freely, without squeezing.\n3. Draw blood exactly to the **20 µL mark**. Wipe the outside of the pipette tip.\n4. Blow the blood gently into the acid at the bottom of the tube. Rinse the pipette by drawing up and expelling the acid 2–3 times. Mix with the stirrer.\n5. **Wait for 10 minutes** for full conversion to acid haematin. Conversion is maximal after ≈ 10 min; the colour keeps changing slowly afterwards.\n6. Add distilled water **drop by drop**, mixing each time, until the colour matches the standard against diffuse daylight. Remove the stirrer before each reading.\n7. Note the reading at the **lower meniscus**. Add one more drop: if the colour becomes lighter, the earlier reading is correct.\n\n## Normal values\n- Adult men **14–18 g/dL** (≈ 13–17).\n- Adult women **12–16 g/dL** (≈ 12–15).\n- **Newborn 16–20 g/dL** (up to ≈ 23).\n- Children (1 yr) ≈ 11–13 g/dL.\n- **WHO anaemia thresholds**: men < 13, non-pregnant women < 12, **pregnant women < 11**, children 6–59 months < 11 g/dL.\n- Anaemia is very common in India: NFHS-5 shows ≈ 57% of women and ≈ 67% of children (6–59 months) anaemic. **Anaemia Mukt Bharat**.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Inherent**: acid haematin is not a true solution (a colloid). **Visual colour matching is subjective**, and colour blindness or poor lighting affects it. The standard fades with time.\n- **Accuracy is only ≈ ±10%**.\n- It **does not measure** carboxy-Hb, met-Hb or sulph-Hb (these are not converted).\n- Fetal Hb resists conversion.\n- **Technical errors**:\n  - incorrect blood volume (air bubbles, squeezing the finger: tissue fluid dilutes the sample)\n  - clotting in the pipette\n  - reading **before 10 min** (falsely low)\n  - a dirty tube; adding too much water at once\n  - parallax at the meniscus\n  - high WBC counts or lipaemia (turbidity)\n\n## Other methods\n- **Cyanmethaemoglobin (Drabkin's) method**: the **ICSH reference/gold standard**. Drabkin's reagent (potassium ferricyanide + potassium cyanide) converts all forms except sulph-Hb to cyanmet-Hb, read in a **photoelectric colorimeter at 540 nm**.\n- Automated haematology analysers (SLS-Hb).\n- **HemoCue** (point of care, used in Anaemia Mukt Bharat).\n- WHO colour scale.\n- Copper sulphate (specific-gravity) method for blood-donor screening: a drop sinks in CuSO₄ of SG 1.053 if Hb ≥ 12.5 g/dL.\n- Oxyhaemoglobin and alkaline haematin methods; Tallqvist paper (obsolete).\n\n## Precautions\n- Use aseptic technique and dispose of sharps correctly.\n- **Never mouth-pipette** in modern practice (use a rubber teat/aspirator).\n- Read the meniscus at eye level in daylight.\n\n## Clinical significance\nHb with RBC count and PCV gives the **red-cell indices**:\n- **MCH = Hb/RBC × 10**\n- **MCHC = Hb/PCV × 100** (normal 32–36 g/dL)\n\nThese classify anaemia (microcytic hypochromic: iron deficiency; macrocytic: B₁₂/folate). Polycythaemia occurs at altitude and in COPD and polycythaemia vera.\n\n**Colour index** = Hb% ÷ RBC% (normal ≈ 0.85–1.15; a historical index).","q":{"b":["N/10 HCl converts Hb to acid haematin.","Pipette 20 µL blood into acid up to the 2 g% mark.","Wait 10 minutes before matching.","Dilute drop by drop; read the lower meniscus.","Men 14–18, women 12–16 g/dL.","WHO anaemia: men < 13, women < 12, pregnancy < 11.","Error ≈ ±10%; visual and subjective.","Does not measure COHb, MetHb, SulphHb.","Squeezing the finger dilutes blood – falsely low.","Gold standard – cyanmethaemoglobin (540 nm).","MCHC = Hb/PCV × 100 (32–36).","CuSO₄ SG 1.053 for donor screening."],"n":["20 µL blood","N/10 HCl","10 min","Men 14–18 g/dL","Women 12–16 g/dL","Error ±10%"],"m":"**\"Sahli takes Ten (minutes) in Tenth-normal HCl\"**.","v":["Reagent in Sahli's method? N/10 hydrochloric acid.","Reference method for Hb? Cyanmethaemoglobin.","Why wait 10 minutes? For complete conversion to acid haematin."]}},"pr|Blood grouping (slide method)":{"d":"## Principle\n**Haemagglutination**: red cells carrying an antigen clump when mixed with the corresponding antibody (antiserum).\n- **Forward (cell) grouping** tests the patient's RBCs with known **anti-A, anti-B and anti-D** sera.\n- **Reverse (serum) grouping** tests the patient's serum with known A and B cells. It is done in blood banks; Landsteiner's law makes the antibodies predictable.\n- **ABO antibodies** are naturally occurring **IgM**, so agglutination is visible at room temperature.\n\n## Requirements\n- Clean glass slide (or white tile) marked A, B, D.\n- Antisera: anti-A (**blue**), anti-B (**yellow**), anti-D (colourless). Modern ones are monoclonal.\n- Lancet, swabs, applicator sticks.\n- Saline (to make a ≈ 5–10% cell suspension, optional).\n\n## Procedure\n1. Place one drop each of anti-A, anti-B and anti-D on the marked areas.\n2. Under aseptic precautions, take a finger-prick and add a small drop of blood (or cell suspension) beside each antiserum. **Blood should be ≈ 1/10th of the antiserum volume**; excess cells can mask or give false results.\n3. Mix each with a **separate clean stick** to avoid cross-contamination. Spread to a ≈ 1.5 cm circle.\n4. Rock the slide gently for **≈ 2 min** for ABO; read Rh at about 2 min (Rh antisera need warmth or a longer time; the slide test for D is done on a warm view-box at ≈ 40 °C).\n5. Look for agglutination with the naked eye, then confirm under the **low-power microscope**. Agglutination shows clumps with a clear background; non-agglutinated cells stay uniformly dispersed.\n\n## Interpretation\n- Agglutination with anti-A only: **A**.\n- Anti-B only: **B**.\n- Both: **AB**.\n- Neither: **O**.\n- With anti-D: **Rh positive**; without: Rh negative.\n\n## Normal frequencies (India, approximate)\n- **B ≈ 32–38%** (the commonest in north India)\n- **O ≈ 29–37%** (commonest in south India)\n- **A ≈ 21–24%**\n- **AB ≈ 6–9%**\n- **Rh negative ≈ 5–6%** (≈ 15% in Caucasians)\n- **Bombay phenotype (Oh)** was first described in Mumbai (1952). These people lack the H antigen, so they make anti-A, anti-B **and anti-H**. They type as O on forward grouping but are incompatible with O blood; reverse grouping and anti-H testing reveal them.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **False positive**:\n  - **rouleaux** (high plasma proteins: myeloma, pregnancy). Dispersed by adding saline.\n  - **drying** of the drop at the edges (looks like clumps)\n  - contaminated reagents or dirty slides\n  - autoagglutinins (cold), Wharton's jelly in cord blood\n  - **pseudoagglutination**\n- **False negative**:\n  - **expired or weak antisera**\n  - **too many cells** (antigen excess) or too few\n  - reading too early\n  - weak subgroups (**A₂, A₂B**) and **weak D (Du)**, which needs an indirect antiglobulin test\n  - the same stick used for all drops (contamination)\n- Clerical/labelling errors are **the commonest cause of fatal transfusion reactions**.\n- Slide grouping is a **screening** method. **Tube grouping** (forward + reverse), column agglutination (gel card) and **cross-matching** are mandatory before transfusion.\n\n## Cross-matching\n- **Major cross-match**: donor cells + recipient serum. The most important.\n- **Minor**: donor serum + recipient cells.\n- Done in saline at room temperature, in albumin at 37 °C, and by **indirect Coombs (antiglobulin)** test for IgG antibodies (Rh, Kell, Duffy, Kidd).\n\n## Clinical importance\n- **Transfusion**: **O negative** is the universal donor (packed cells) and **AB positive** the universal recipient (theoretical). In practice group-specific blood is given.\n- **Haemolytic disease of the newborn** (Rh-negative mother, Rh-positive fetus; second pregnancy). Prevented by **anti-D immunoglobulin** (300 µg) within 72 h of delivery and at 28 weeks antenatally.\n- ABO incompatibility in HDN is milder and can occur in the first pregnancy (O mother, A/B baby; IgG anti-A/B).\n- **Indian donor criteria** (NBTC/DGHS): age 18–65, weight ≥ 45 kg (≥ 55 kg for a 450 mL donation), **Hb ≥ 12.5 g/dL**, interval ≥ 3 months (men) / 4 months (women).\n- Disputed paternity (now DNA), forensic use, organ transplantation (ABO compatibility), disease associations (O: peptic ulcer; A: gastric cancer).","q":{"b":["Principle – antigen–antibody agglutination.","Anti-A blue, anti-B yellow, anti-D colourless.","Blood ≈ 1/10th the volume of antiserum.","Separate stick for each drop.","ABO antibodies – natural IgM.","Commonest in India: B (north) and O (south).","Rh negative ≈ 5–6% in India.","Bombay phenotype – no H antigen; anti-H in serum.","Rouleaux and drying → false positive.","Weak antisera, A₂ subgroup, weak D → false negative.","Clerical error – commonest cause of fatal reactions.","Major cross-match: donor cells + recipient serum.","Anti-D within 72 h prevents Rh HDN."],"n":["Rh− ≈ 5–6% India","Donor Hb ≥ 12.5 g/dL","Donor weight ≥ 45 kg","Anti-D within 72 h"],"m":"Antisera colours: **\"A = Azure (blue), B = Banana (yellow)\"**.","v":["Universal donor? O negative.","What is Bombay blood group? Absence of H antigen (Oh).","Which cross-match is more important? Major cross-match."]}},"pr|RBC count (haemocytometer)":{"d":"## Principle\nBlood is diluted a known amount (**1 in 200**) with an isotonic diluting fluid that preserves red cells. The cells are counted in a known volume of the **Neubauer counting chamber**, and the count per µL (mm³) is calculated.\n\n## Apparatus\n- **Improved Neubauer chamber**: depth **0.1 mm**. The ruled area is 3 × 3 mm, i.e. 9 large squares of 1 mm².\n  - The **central 1 mm² square** is divided into **25 medium squares** (each 0.2 × 0.2 mm), each further divided into **16 small squares** (1/400 mm² each), giving 400 small squares.\n  - The 4 corner large squares are divided into 16 squares each (for WBC).\n- **Special coverslip** (optically flat).\n- **RBC pipette (Thoma)**: marks at 0.5, 1 and **101**, with a **red bead** in the bulb.\n  - Bulb volume is 100 units.\n  - Drawing blood to 0.5 and fluid to 101 gives a dilution of 0.5 in 100 = **1:200**. (The stem fluid, 1 unit, does not mix.)\n- **Hayem's fluid**: NaCl (isotonicity), Na₂SO₄ (prevents rouleaux), HgCl₂ (preservative/antifungal), distilled water. Alternatives: **Gower's** or formol-citrate (Dacie's) fluid, or isotonic saline.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Aseptic finger-prick. Draw blood **exactly to 0.5**. Wipe the tip.\n2. Draw diluting fluid to the **101 mark**, rotating the pipette to mix.\n3. Shake for **2–3 min** (figure-of-eight).\n4. **Discard the first 2–3 drops** (the stem contains only diluting fluid).\n5. **Charge the chamber**: touch the tip to the edge of the coverslip and let fluid run under by capillarity, without overflow or air bubbles.\n6. Wait **2–3 min** for the cells to settle.\n7. Under the **high-power (40×)** objective, count RBCs in **5 medium squares**: the 4 corner squares and the central one of the central 1 mm² area. That is **80 small squares**.\n8. **Counting rule** (\"L rule\"): count cells touching the **top and left** borders; ignore those on the bottom and right, to avoid double counting.\n\n## Calculation\n- Volume counted = 80 × (1/400 mm²) × 0.1 mm = **1/50 mm³ (0.02 µL)**.\n- RBC/µL = N × dilution × (1/volume) = N × 200 × 50 = **N × 10,000**.\n- Example: N = 500 gives 5,000,000 /µL (5 million/mm³).\n\n## Normal values\n- **Men 4.5–6.0 (≈ 5 ± 0.5) million/µL**.\n- **Women 4.0–5.5 (≈ 4.5 ± 0.5) million/µL**.\n- **Newborn 6–7 million/µL**.\n\nPhysiological variations:\n- ↑ at **high altitude**, in newborns, in men, after exercise, and in dehydration (relative).\n- ↓ in pregnancy (haemodilution) and in the elderly.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Pipette errors**: wrong volume (air bubbles, blood not at the exact mark), blood clotting in the pipette, a wet or dirty pipette, not discarding the first drops, inadequate mixing.\n- **Chamber errors**:\n  - **overcharging** (fluid in the moat; increases depth)\n  - undercharging, air bubbles\n  - a dirty chamber or coverslip\n  - an ordinary coverslip\n  - uneven distribution of cells\n- **Counting errors**: not applying the border rule, too little time to settle, fatigue.\n- **Inherent statistical (Poisson) error**: the coefficient of variation is ≈ **±8–10%** even with good technique, less with more cells counted (CV ≈ 1/√N). That is why counting more squares or duplicate counts improves accuracy.\n- Squeezing the finger dilutes blood with tissue fluid (falsely low).\n- Very high WBC counts (leukaemia) can be counted as RBCs.\n- **Automated electronic counters** (Coulter principle: impedance) are more precise (CV ≈ 1–2%).\n\n## Clinical significance\n- Diagnosis of anaemia and **polycythaemia** (vera; secondary to altitude, COPD, cyanotic heart disease).\n- Red cell indices:\n  - **MCV = PCV × 10 / RBC (millions)**, normal 80–100 fL\n  - **MCH = Hb × 10 / RBC**, normal 27–32 pg\n- Response to therapy.\n\n## Precautions\n- Clean, dry apparatus; rinse the pipette with water, alcohol and ether after use.\n- Do not touch the ruled area.\n- Dispose of lancets in a sharps container.","q":{"b":["Dilution 1:200 (blood to 0.5, fluid to 101).","Red bead in the RBC pipette.","Hayem's fluid: NaCl, Na₂SO₄, HgCl₂.","Chamber depth 0.1 mm.","Count 5 medium (80 small) squares under high power.","Volume counted = 1/50 mm³.","RBC/µL = N × 10,000.","Men 4.5–6.0, women 4.0–5.5 million/µL.","Count top and left borders; ignore bottom and right.","Discard first drops – stem fluid.","Overcharging and air bubbles cause error.","Statistical error ≈ ±8–10%.","MCV = PCV × 10/RBC (80–100 fL)."],"n":["Dilution 1:200","Depth 0.1 mm","Volume 1/50 mm³","N × 10,000","Men 5 ± 0.5 million"],"m":"RBC counting **\"5 squares, 200 dilution, × 10,000\"**.","v":["Dilution for RBC count? 1:200.","Why discard the first drops? They contain only diluting fluid from the stem.","Which constituent of Hayem's prevents rouleaux? Sodium sulphate."]}},"pr|WBC (total leucocyte) count":{"d":"## Principle\nBlood is diluted **1 in 20** with **Turk's fluid**. This **lyses the RBCs** (glacial acetic acid) and **stains the WBC nuclei** (gentian violet), so leucocytes can be counted in a known volume of the Neubauer chamber.\n\n## Apparatus\n- **WBC pipette**: marks 0.5, 1 and **11**, with a **white bead**. Bulb volume 10 units.\n  - Blood to 0.5, fluid to 11 → 0.5 in 10 = **1:20**.\n  - Blood to 1, fluid to 11 → 1:10 (used when the count is low).\n- **Turk's fluid**: **glacial acetic acid 1.5–3%** (lyses RBCs), **gentian violet 1%** (stains nuclei violet), distilled water.\n- Improved Neubauer chamber and coverslip, microscope, lancet, spirit.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Aseptic finger-prick; wipe the first drop.\n2. Draw blood to the **0.5 mark** and Turk's fluid to **11**.\n3. Mix by rolling for **2–3 min**. Allow time for RBC lysis.\n4. **Discard the first 2–3 drops**.\n5. Charge the chamber without overflow or bubbles. Let it settle for **2–3 min**.\n6. Under **low power (10×)**, count WBCs in the **4 corner large squares** (each 1 mm², divided into 16). WBCs appear as **refractile dark-violet dots with a halo**.\n7. Apply the same **border (\"L\") rule**: count cells on the top and left lines.\n\n## Calculation\n- Volume counted = 4 × 1 mm² × 0.1 mm = **0.4 mm³**.\n- WBC/µL = N × 20 / 0.4 = **N × 50**.\n- Example: N = 150 gives 7,500 /µL.\n\n## Normal values\n- **Adults 4,000–11,000 /µL** (4–11 × 10⁹/L).\n- Newborn 10,000–25,000 /µL.\n- Infants ≈ 6,000–18,000 /µL.\n\n**Physiological variations**:\n- ↑ after exercise, meals, emotion/stress, pregnancy and labour, in the newborn, and in the afternoon (diurnal). Exercise and stress cause demargination (adrenaline, cortisol).\n- ↓ in the early morning.\n\n## Terms\n- **Leucocytosis > 11,000**:\n  - bacterial infections (neutrophilia)\n  - inflammation, tissue necrosis (MI, burns)\n  - haemorrhage, steroids\n  - **leukaemia** (very high: ≥ 50,000–100,000 or more)\n  - **leukaemoid reaction** (> 50,000 with immature forms but benign: severe infection, TB; LAP score high)\n- **Leucopenia < 4,000**:\n  - viral infections, **typhoid**, **dengue** (with thrombocytopenia: common in India), **malaria**, kala-azar\n  - aplastic anaemia, **chemotherapy/radiation**, drugs (chloramphenicol, carbimazole)\n  - hypersplenism, B₁₂/folate deficiency, SLE, HIV, overwhelming sepsis\n- **Agranulocytosis**: absolute neutrophil count < 500 /µL (severe).\n\n## Corrected WBC count\n**Nucleated RBCs** (newborn, haemolysis, leukaemia) are not lysed by Turk's fluid and are counted as WBCs.\n- Corrected WBC = total count × 100 / (100 + nRBCs per 100 WBC) from the smear.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Pipette**: wrong volume, bubbles, clotting, inadequate mixing, wet pipette, first drops not discarded, mixing up the RBC and WBC pipettes.\n- **Chamber**: over- or undercharging, bubbles, uneven distribution, dirt or yeast in the fluid (old Turk's fluid; filter it).\n- **Counting**: not following the border rule, confusing debris with cells, too little settling time.\n- **Statistical error ≈ ±15–20%** because fewer cells are counted (≈ 100–200). Counting more squares (all 9) or duplicates improves it.\n- Squeezing the finger; specimen taken after exercise or a meal.\n- Electronic counters (impedance/flow cytometry) are more precise and also give the differential.\n\n## Precautions\nClean and dry pipettes, aseptic technique, safe disposal of sharps, and standard conditions (preferably morning, at rest).\n\n## Clinical significance\nDiagnosis of infection, leukaemia and drug toxicity. **Monitoring chemotherapy** (stop if neutrophils < 1,500). Dengue management (NVBDCP). Prognosis in sepsis.\n\n**Absolute counts** = TLC × % from the DLC; these are more meaningful than percentages.","q":{"b":["Dilution 1:20 (blood to 0.5, fluid to 11).","White bead in the WBC pipette.","Turk's fluid: glacial acetic acid (lyses RBC) + gentian violet (stains nuclei).","Count 4 corner large squares under low power.","Volume 0.4 mm³; WBC/µL = N × 50.","Normal 4,000–11,000 /µL.","Newborn 10,000–25,000 /µL.","Leucocytosis – bacterial infection, leukaemia, stress.","Leucopenia – typhoid, dengue, malaria, viral, drugs.","Nucleated RBCs counted as WBCs – correct the count.","Statistical error ≈ ±15–20%.","Leukaemoid reaction > 50,000 but benign.","Absolute count = TLC × % from DLC."],"n":["Dilution 1:20","Volume 0.4 mm³","N × 50","4,000–11,000 /µL","Agranulocytosis ANC < 500"],"m":"**\"WBC – White bead, Turk's, × 50\"**.","v":["Function of acetic acid in Turk's fluid? Lyses red cells.","Multiplication factor for TLC? 50.","Leucopenia in which Indian fevers? Typhoid, dengue, malaria."]}},"pr|Differential leucocyte count":{"d":"## Principle\nA thin blood smear is stained with a **Romanowsky stain** (Leishman's, Giemsa, Wright's, Field's). The stain contains **eosin (acidic, stains basic components such as Hb and eosinophil granules pink-red)** and **methylene blue and its oxidation products (azures; basic, stain acidic nuclei and RNA blue-purple)**. This combination gives differential staining.\n\n**100 consecutive leucocytes** are classified under **oil immersion**, and each type is expressed as a percentage.\n\n## Making the smear\n1. Put a small drop of blood ≈ 1 cm from one end of a clean, grease-free slide.\n2. Hold the spreader at **30–45°**, touch the drop and let it spread along the edge.\n3. Push forward smoothly and quickly.\n4. A good smear is **two-thirds to three-quarters of the slide**, with a **tongue-shaped tail**, smooth and even, no lines or holes.\n\nAir dry quickly by waving.\n\n## Leishman staining\n1. Cover the smear with **Leishman's stain** (in **methanol**, which **fixes**) for **2 min**.\n2. Add **double the volume of buffered distilled water (pH 6.8)** and mix by blowing. A greenish metallic scum forms. Leave for **8–10 min** (staining).\n3. Wash with a gentle stream of water (not flooding) until the smear is **pinkish**. Air dry and examine.\n\n## Examination\n- Low power first to choose an area, then **oil immersion (100×)**.\n- Count in the **body/tail junction** where cells are just touching.\n- Follow a **\"battlement\" (zig-zag) track** to avoid bias: neutrophils and monocytes drift to the edges and tail, lymphocytes to the centre.\n- Use a tally or counter for 100 cells (200 for more accuracy).\n\n## Identification\n- **Neutrophil** (10–12 µm): nucleus with **2–5 lobes** joined by chromatin threads; fine **pink-violet (neutral) granules**. Barr body (\"drumstick\") in ≈ 3% of female neutrophils.\n- **Eosinophil** (10–14 µm): **bilobed** (\"spectacle\") nucleus; large **orange-red granules** filling the cytoplasm.\n- **Basophil** (8–10 µm): S-shaped nucleus **obscured by coarse dark-blue/purple granules**. Rarest.\n- **Lymphocyte**:\n  - small (7–10 µm): large round dark nucleus, thin rim of sky-blue cytoplasm\n  - large (12–16 µm): more cytoplasm\n- **Monocyte** (14–20 µm, the largest): **kidney- or horseshoe-shaped** nucleus, abundant **grey-blue (\"ground glass\") cytoplasm**, sometimes vacuoles.\n\n## Normal values (adults)\n- **Neutrophils 40–75%** (2,000–7,500 /µL)\n- **Lymphocytes 20–40% (up to 45)** (1,500–4,000)\n- **Monocytes 2–8%** (200–800)\n- **Eosinophils 1–6%** (40–440)\n- **Basophils 0–1%**\n\nIn **children up to ≈ 4–5 years, lymphocytes predominate**. Crossovers occur at ≈ 4–7 days and ≈ 4–7 years.\n\n**Arneth count**: neutrophils grouped by number of lobes (1 to 5). **Shift to the left** (immature, band forms) in acute infection; **shift to the right** (hypersegmented ≥ 5 lobes) in B₁₂/folate deficiency.\n\n## Variations\n- **Neutrophilia**: acute bacterial infections, MI, burns, steroids, CML.\n- **Neutropenia**: typhoid, viral fever, drugs, aplastic anaemia.\n- **Lymphocytosis**: viral infections, TB, whooping cough, CLL.\n- **Eosinophilia**: **\"NAACP\"**: Neoplasm, Allergy (asthma), Addison's, Collagen vascular disease, **Parasites** (helminths; very common in India; **tropical pulmonary eosinophilia**, filarial).\n- **Monocytosis**: TB, malaria, kala-azar, SABE, monocytic leukaemia.\n- **Basophilia**: CML, myeloproliferative disorders, hypothyroidism.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Smear**: thick or uneven (cells overlap), lines from a chipped spreader, delayed drying (crenation), a greasy slide.\n- **Staining**: too acidic (everything red; pH < 6.8) or too alkaline (everything blue). Stain precipitate (unfiltered stain or drying). Under- or over-staining. Inadequate washing.\n- **Counting**: **uneven distribution** (count in the wrong zone). Counting fewer than 100 cells. Misidentification (large lymphocyte vs monocyte). Smudge cells (CLL).\n- **Statistical error** for rare cells (basophils, eosinophils) is large when only 100 cells are counted.\n- **EDTA artefacts** if the smear is delayed more than ≈ 2–3 h.\n\n## Other uses of the smear\n- RBC morphology (anisocytosis, poikilocytosis, hypochromia)\n- Platelet estimate\n- **Malarial parasites** (thick and thin films)\n- Microfilaria (night smear)\n- Abnormal cells (blasts)","q":{"b":["Romanowsky stain: eosin (acidic) + methylene blue (basic).","Leishman 2 min, then double water 8–10 min.","Methanol in Leishman fixes the smear.","Buffer pH 6.8.","Count 100 cells under oil immersion, battlement track.","N 40–75, L 20–40, M 2–8, E 1–6, B 0–1%.","Monocyte – largest; kidney-shaped nucleus.","Basophil – rarest; nucleus obscured by granules.","Children < 5 yr: lymphocytes predominate.","Left shift – infection; right shift – B₁₂/folate deficiency.","Eosinophilia – parasites, allergy (NAACP).","Acid stain → too red; alkaline → too blue.","Smear 2/3–3/4 of the slide with a tongue-shaped tail."],"n":["N 40–75%","L 20–40%","M 2–8%","E 1–6%","B 0–1%","Spreader 30–45°"],"m":"Order of frequency: **\"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas\"** – N > L > M > E > B.","v":["Fixative in Leishman's stain? Methanol.","Leucocyte increased in helminthic infections? Eosinophil.","Largest leucocyte? Monocyte."]}},"pr|Bleeding time and clotting time":{"d":"## Bleeding time (BT)\n### Principle\nThe time from a standard skin puncture until bleeding stops. It tests **platelet number and function** (plug formation) and **capillary integrity (vascular response)**. It is a test of **primary haemostasis**, not of coagulation factors.\n\n### Duke's method\n1. Clean the **ear lobe** or fingertip with spirit and let it dry. Do not squeeze.\n2. Make a puncture ≈ 3 mm deep with a sterile lancet and **start the stopwatch**.\n3. **Every 15 (or 30) seconds** blot the blood with filter paper, without touching the wound.\n4. Stop the watch when no more blood stains the paper.\n5. BT = number of spots × 15 s (or read from the watch).\n- **Normal 1–5 min** (Duke: ≈ 1–3 min).\n\n### Ivy's method (more standardised)\n1. Apply a BP cuff on the arm at **40 mmHg**.\n2. Make 2–3 standard incisions on the volar forearm (template device, e.g. 10 mm × 1 mm, avoiding veins).\n3. Blot every 30 s.\n- **Normal 2–7 min** (up to ≈ 9 with the template).\n\n### Prolonged BT\n- **Thrombocytopenia** (< 50,000–100,000 /µL): ITP, dengue, aplastic anaemia, leukaemia, DIC.\n- **Platelet dysfunction**: **aspirin/NSAIDs** (stop 7–10 days before the test), uraemia, Glanzmann's thrombasthenia (GpIIb/IIIa), Bernard–Soulier (GpIb).\n- **von Willebrand disease** (BT ↑, often aPTT ↑ too).\n- Vascular disorders: scurvy, Henoch–Schönlein purpura, Ehlers–Danlos.\n- **Normal in haemophilia** (classic viva).\n\n### Sources of error\n- Variable depth and size of the wound (Duke).\n- Touching the wound with the filter paper (dislodges the plug).\n- Squeezing, a cold or vasoconstricted ear, alcohol not dried.\n- Drugs (aspirin).\n- Inter-observer variation.\n\nBT is **poorly reproducible** and a poor predictor of surgical bleeding, so it is now largely replaced by **PFA-100** and platelet aggregation studies. It is still taught for CBME skill certification.\n\n## Clotting time (CT)\n### Principle\nThe time taken for blood, once outside the body and in contact with glass, to clot. It tests the **intrinsic and common pathways** (factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, fibrinogen) and is insensitive to mild deficiency.\n\n### Capillary tube method (Wright's)\n1. Aseptic deep finger-prick; free-flowing blood; **start the stopwatch when blood first appears**.\n2. Fill **capillary tubes** (≈ 10 cm long, 1–1.5 mm bore) by tilting.\n3. Hold the tube between the palms (≈ body temperature). **After 2 min, break off ≈ 1 cm every 30 s**, pulling the pieces gently apart.\n4. Stop the watch when a **fibrin thread** forms between the broken ends.\n- **Normal 3–8 min** (≈ 4–6; textbook values vary).\n\n### Lee–White test-tube method (more reliable)\n1. Put 1 mL of venous blood in each of 3 glass tubes at **37 °C**.\n2. Tilt the tubes every 30 s.\n3. CT is the point when the tube can be inverted without the blood flowing.\n- **Normal 5–10 (up to 15) min**.\n\n### Prolonged CT\n- **Haemophilia A (VIII) and B (IX)**: only in severe deficiency (< 1–2%). **BT normal, CT prolonged.**\n- **Anticoagulants**: heparin. CT was once used to monitor it; now aPTT.\n- Vitamin K deficiency, liver disease (severe), afibrinogenaemia, DIC.\n- Snake bite: **viper envenomation**, common in rural India. The **20-minute whole-blood clotting test (20WBCT)** is the bedside test recommended in Indian guidelines: blood not clotted at 20 min means coagulopathy, so give anti-snake venom.\n\n### Sources of error\n- **Tissue fluid contamination** (squeezing; tissue thromboplastin shortens CT).\n- Temperature (cold prolongs it).\n- Tube diameter and cleanliness (wettable surfaces).\n- Starting the watch late.\n- Breaking pieces too vigorously or too often.\n- Low sensitivity: misses mild factor deficiencies.\n\n## Better tests of coagulation\n- **Prothrombin time (PT/INR)**: extrinsic and common pathway; warfarin monitoring. Normal 11–16 s; INR 0.8–1.2.\n- **aPTT**: intrinsic and common pathway; heparin, haemophilia. Normal 25–40 s.\n- **Thrombin time**, fibrinogen, D-dimer.\n- **Platelet count** (normal **1.5–4.5 lakh/µL**).","q":{"b":["BT tests platelets and vessels; CT tests intrinsic coagulation.","Duke's: ear-lobe prick, blot every 15–30 s.","BT normal 1–5 min (Duke), 2–7 min (Ivy).","Ivy: cuff at 40 mmHg, forearm incisions.","BT ↑ in thrombocytopenia, aspirin, vWD.","Haemophilia: BT normal, CT prolonged.","CT capillary method: break 1 cm every 30 s; fibrin thread.","CT normal 3–8 min (capillary); 5–10 min (Lee–White).","Do not touch the wound while blotting.","Tissue fluid shortens CT.","20WBCT for viper bite in India.","PT – extrinsic; aPTT – intrinsic.","Platelets 1.5–4.5 lakh/µL."],"n":["BT 1–5 min","CT 3–8 min","Ivy cuff 40 mmHg","Platelets 1.5–4.5 lakh","20WBCT 20 min"],"m":"**\"Bleeding = Blood vessels + platelets; Clotting = Coagulation factors\"**.","v":["BT in haemophilia? Normal.","Drug that prolongs BT? Aspirin.","Bedside test for viper envenomation? 20-minute whole-blood clotting test."]}},"pr|ESR and PCV":{"d":"## Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)\n### Principle\nAnticoagulated blood stands in a vertical tube and the RBCs settle under gravity. The **height of the clear plasma column after 1 hour** (mm in the 1st hour) is the ESR.\n\nThree phases:\n1. **Rouleaux formation** (≈ 10 min)\n2. **Rapid settling** (≈ 40 min)\n3. **Packing** (≈ 10 min)\n\nRouleaux formation is the main determinant. Positively charged **plasma proteins (fibrinogen above all, then globulins)** neutralise the RBC negative surface charge (zeta potential), so RBCs stack and sink faster (larger mass relative to surface: Stokes' law).\n\n### Westergren method (ICSH reference)\n- **Westergren pipette**: 300 mm long, 2.5 mm bore, graduated **0–200 mm**.\n- Anticoagulant **3.8% trisodium citrate**, **1 part : 4 parts blood** (0.4 mL + 1.6 mL). EDTA blood diluted 4:1 with citrate or saline is also accepted.\n- Steps:\n  1. Fill to the 0 mark without bubbles.\n  2. Set **exactly vertical** in the stand at room temperature (18–25 °C), away from vibration and sunlight.\n  3. Read the plasma column at exactly **1 hour**.\n- **Normal (Westergren)**:\n  - **men 0–15 mm/h** (rule: age/2)\n  - **women 0–20 mm/h** ((age + 10)/2)\n  - children 3–13 mm/h\n  - higher in the elderly\n- **Wintrobe method**: 110 mm tube, 3 mm bore, graduated 0–100 mm; **double oxalate** (Wintrobe's mixture: ammonium + potassium oxalate) or EDTA, undiluted. **The same tube gives PCV.** Normal ESR (Wintrobe): men 0–9, women 0–20 mm/h.\n\n### Factors affecting ESR\n- **↑ESR**:\n  - **↑fibrinogen/globulins**: infections (**TB**, very relevant in India), inflammation (rheumatoid arthritis, rheumatic fever), MI, **multiple myeloma** (very high, > 100), malignancy, temporal arteritis, polymyalgia rheumatica\n  - **anaemia** (fewer cells, faster fall), **pregnancy** (≥ 3rd month), menstruation, old age\n  - macrocytes; tilted tube; high room temperature\n- **↓ESR**:\n  - **polycythaemia**\n  - **sickle cell** disease and **spherocytosis** (abnormal shapes prevent rouleaux)\n  - **afibrinogenaemia**, CHF, hypoalbuminaemia (some)\n  - cold, delayed or short reading\n  - excess anticoagulant; microcytes\n- ESR is **non-specific**. It is used to **monitor** disease activity (TB treatment, RA, temporal arteritis) more than to diagnose. CRP is a more rapid and specific marker.\n\n### Sources of error\n- **Tube not vertical** (a 3° tilt can raise ESR ≈ 30%).\n- Wrong anticoagulant ratio.\n- Bubbles or a clotted sample.\n- Delay > 2–4 h (at room temperature) before setting up.\n- Temperature, vibration.\n- Reading at the wrong time or at the wrong level (a hazy buffy-coat line).\n- Haemolysis.\n\n## Packed cell volume (PCV, haematocrit)\n### Principle\nAnticoagulated blood is **centrifuged** so the cells pack down. PCV is the **percentage of blood volume occupied by RBCs**.\n\n### Methods\n- **Wintrobe macro method**:\n  1. Fill the Wintrobe tube to the 100 mark with oxalated/EDTA blood (using a long Pasteur pipette from the bottom up, to avoid bubbles).\n  2. Centrifuge at **≈ 3000 rpm (≈ 2,000–2,300 g) for 30 min**.\n  3. Read the height of the red column. Above it is the **buffy coat** (WBCs and platelets, ≈ 1 mm), then plasma.\n- **Microhaematocrit** (preferred):\n  1. Use a heparinised capillary tube (75 mm), seal one end with clay.\n  2. Centrifuge at **≈ 10,000–12,000 g for 5 min**.\n  3. Read with a microhaematocrit reader.\n- Automated analysers *calculate* Hct = MCV × RBC.\n\n### Normal values\n- **Men 40–54% (≈ 45 ± 5)**.\n- **Women 36–47% (≈ 41 ± 5)**.\n- **Newborn 44–64% (≈ 55)**.\n\n### Variations\n- **↑PCV**: polycythaemia (vera, altitude, COPD, cyanotic heart disease), **dehydration/haemoconcentration**: vomiting, diarrhoea, burns, **dengue plasma leakage** (a ≥ 20% rise in Hct is a warning sign).\n- **↓PCV**: anaemia, pregnancy (haemodilution), overhydration, haemorrhage (after hours, once haemodilution occurs).\n\n### Sources of error\n- **Trapped plasma** (≈ 1–3%; more in macro, sickle cells and spherocytes): PCV falsely high.\n- Inadequate centrifugation time or speed: falsely high.\n- Wrong anticoagulant (excess EDTA shrinks cells; oxalate alone shrinks them, hence the mixture).\n- Haemolysis, bubbles.\n- Misreading the buffy coat.\n- Prolonged tourniquet causes haemoconcentration.\n\n### Uses\n- **Red cell indices**:\n  - **MCV = PCV × 10/RBC (fL)**\n  - **MCHC = Hb × 100/PCV (32–36 g/dL)**\n- Blood viscosity.\n- **Buffy-coat** examination (QBC for malaria, smears for blasts).\n- **Plasma colour** (icteric, haemolysed, lipaemic).","q":{"b":["ESR depends mainly on rouleaux formation.","Fibrinogen is the main protein raising ESR.","Westergren: 200 mm scale, 3.8% citrate 1:4.","ESR men 0–15, women 0–20 mm/h (Westergren).","ESR ↑ TB, myeloma, anaemia, pregnancy.","ESR ↓ polycythaemia, sickle cells, spherocytes.","Tube must be vertical; tilt raises ESR.","Wintrobe tube gives both ESR and PCV.","PCV: centrifuge 3000 rpm for 30 min.","PCV men 40–54%, women 36–47%.","Buffy coat – WBCs and platelets.","Dengue: Hct rise ≥ 20% = plasma leakage.","MCHC = Hb × 100/PCV."],"n":["ESR M 0–15, F 0–20","Citrate 1:4","PCV M 40–54%","PCV F 36–47%","3000 rpm × 30 min"],"m":"ESR rule: **\"Men age/2, Women (age + 10)/2\"**.","v":["Main factor in ESR? Rouleaux formation (fibrinogen).","Why is ESR low in polycythaemia? Increased RBCs hinder sedimentation.","What is the buffy coat? Layer of WBCs and platelets."]}},"pr|Osmotic fragility & other haematology":{"d":"## Osmotic fragility test\n### Principle\nRBCs placed in **hypotonic saline** swell as water enters, change from biconcave to spherical, and then **haemolyse** when the membrane stretches beyond its limit.\n- **Osmotic fragility** is the susceptibility to this lysis. It depends on the **surface-area-to-volume ratio**: spherocytes are fragile, thin flat cells are resistant.\n\n### Procedure\n1. Set up a series of 12–13 tubes with NaCl from **0.9% down to 0.1%** (e.g. 0.9, 0.8, 0.7, 0.6, 0.55, 0.5, 0.45, 0.4, 0.35, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1) and distilled water. Typically 2–5 mL in each.\n2. Add **one drop of fresh heparinised blood** (not EDTA/oxalate, which alter the result) to each. Mix gently.\n3. Leave at room temperature for **30 min to 1 h**, then centrifuge (or let settle).\n4. Look for a red supernatant (haemolysis) vs a clear supernatant with a red button.\n5. For quantitative work, read in a colorimeter at 540 nm and plot a curve of % haemolysis vs NaCl concentration.\n\n### Normal values\n- **Haemolysis begins at ≈ 0.45–0.50% NaCl** (minimum resistance).\n- **Complete at ≈ 0.30–0.35%** (maximum resistance).\n- Distilled water = 100%.\n- The **MCF (median corpuscular fragility**, 50% lysis) ≈ 0.40–0.45%.\n\n### Increased fragility (curve shifted right)\n- **Hereditary spherocytosis** (lysis begins at ≈ 0.6–0.7%). Better seen after **24 h incubation at 37 °C**.\n- Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (spherocytes).\n- Burns.\n\n### Decreased fragility (shifted left)\n- **Thalassaemia** (common in India: **β-thalassaemia trait ≈ 3–4%**). The **NESTROFT** test is the Indian screening test (single-tube 0.36% buffered saline; a positive test means the newsprint cannot be read).\n- **Iron deficiency** (thin target cells)\n- **Sickle cell** disease\n- Obstructive jaundice and liver disease (target cells)\n- Post-splenectomy\n\n### Sources of error\n- Inaccurate NaCl concentrations (weigh carefully; check with an osmometer).\n- Wrong anticoagulant.\n- Excess blood.\n- Temperature and pH variations.\n- Delay (blood stored for long becomes more fragile).\n- Mechanical haemolysis by vigorous mixing.\n- Reading by eye is subjective.\n\n## Reticulocyte count\n- **Supravital stain** (new methylene blue or brilliant cresyl blue) precipitates ribosomal RNA as a **blue reticulum**.\n- Count per 1000 RBCs.\n- **Normal 0.5–2.5%** in adults; **2–6% in the newborn**.\n- Corrected reticulocyte count = % × (patient PCV/45).\n- Reflects **bone marrow erythropoietic activity**:\n  - ↑ after haemorrhage, haemolysis, and response to iron/B₁₂ therapy (reticulocyte peak at 5–10 days)\n  - ↓ in aplastic anaemia, renal failure\n\n## Platelet count\n- **Direct method**: Rees–Ecker fluid (sodium citrate, formalin, brilliant cresyl blue) or **1% ammonium oxalate** (lyses RBCs). Dilution 1:200 in the RBC pipette. Count the **25 medium squares (central 1 mm²)** under high power or phase contrast, after letting the chamber stand in a moist chamber for 10–20 min to settle. Platelets/µL = N × 2,000.\n- **Indirect** from the smear (platelets per oil field × 15,000–20,000) is a rough estimate.\n- **Normal 1.5–4.5 lakh/µL** (150–450 × 10⁹/L).\n- **Thrombocytopenia < 1.5 lakh**: dengue (< 20,000 risks bleeding), ITP, aplastic anaemia, DIC, hypersplenism.\n- **Pseudothrombocytopenia**: EDTA-induced clumping.\n- Thrombocytosis: inflammation, iron deficiency, post-splenectomy, essential thrombocythaemia.\n\n## Absolute eosinophil count\n- **Pilot's or Dunger's fluid** (eosin + acetone: stains eosinophils, lyses others); Fuchs–Rosenthal chamber.\n- **Normal 40–440 /µL**.\n- ↑ in allergy, parasites, tropical pulmonary eosinophilia.\n- ↓ with steroids. The basis of the old **Thorn test** for adrenal reserve: ACTH reduces eosinophils by > 50%.\n\n## Other tests (brief)\n- **Specific gravity** of blood: copper sulphate method (blood **≈ 1.050–1.060**, plasma ≈ 1.025–1.029).\n- **Blood indices** from Hb, PCV and RBC.\n- **Sickling test** (sodium metabisulphite) and **solubility test** for HbS (tribal belts of India).\n- **Coombs test**, **haemoglobin electrophoresis/HPLC** (thalassaemia confirmation).","q":{"b":["RBCs swell and lyse in hypotonic saline.","Fragility ∝ sphericity (↓surface area/volume).","Lysis begins 0.45–0.5%, complete 0.3–0.35% NaCl.","↑Fragility – hereditary spherocytosis.","↓Fragility – thalassaemia, iron deficiency, sickle cell.","NESTROFT – Indian screening for β-thalassaemia trait.","Use heparinised fresh blood.","Reticulocytes 0.5–2.5%; supravital new methylene blue.","Reticulocyte peak 5–10 days after haematinics.","Platelets 1.5–4.5 lakh/µL; ammonium oxalate 1:200.","EDTA can cause pseudothrombocytopenia.","Absolute eosinophils 40–440 /µL.","Blood SG ≈ 1.050–1.060."],"n":["Lysis 0.45–0.5%","Complete 0.3–0.35%","Retics 0.5–2.5%","Platelets 1.5–4.5 lakh","AEC 40–440"],"m":"**\"Spheres Split, Targets Tolerate\"** – spherocytes fragile; target cells (thalassaemia) resistant.","v":["Osmotic fragility in hereditary spherocytosis? Increased.","Screening test for thalassaemia trait in India? NESTROFT.","Stain for reticulocytes? New methylene blue (supravital)."]}},"pr|Blood pressure measurement (auscultatory method)":{"d":"## Principle\nRiva-Rocci (1896) introduced the **sphygmomanometer**; **Korotkoff (1905)** described the sounds.\n- An inflatable cuff compresses the **brachial artery**. As cuff pressure is lowered, blood spurts through the partly compressed artery.\n- The resulting **turbulent flow** and vessel-wall vibrations produce the **Korotkoff sounds**, heard with a stethoscope distal to the cuff.\n- **Systolic BP** is the pressure at which the first sound appears. **Diastolic** is where the sounds disappear.\n- It is an **indirect** method. Direct intra-arterial measurement is the reference.\n\n## Apparatus\n- **Mercury sphygmomanometer**: the classical standard, now being phased out (Minamata Convention).\n- Aneroid devices (calibrate regularly) and validated automated oscillometric devices.\n- **Cuff bladder size**:\n  - **length ≥ 80% and width ≈ 40% of arm circumference**\n  - standard adult bladder 12–13 × 22–26 cm; use large or thigh cuffs for obese people and paediatric cuffs for children\n\n## Procedure\n1. Subject **seated** comfortably for **5 min**. No smoking, caffeine or exercise for 30 min. Bladder emptied. Back supported, feet flat, legs uncrossed, no talking.\n2. Arm bare, supported at **heart level** (mid-sternum).\n3. Wrap the cuff snugly, **2–3 cm above the cubital fossa**, with the bladder centre over the brachial artery.\n4. **Palpatory method first**:\n   - palpate the **radial pulse** and inflate until it disappears\n   - deflate slowly; the pressure at which the pulse reappears is the **palpatory systolic**\n   - this **avoids missing the auscultatory gap**\n5. **Auscultatory method**:\n   - place the stethoscope bell/diaphragm lightly over the brachial artery (medial to the biceps tendon), not under the cuff\n   - inflate to **20–30 mmHg above the palpatory systolic**\n   - deflate at **2–3 mmHg per second (per beat)**\n6. Read at eye level (avoid parallax), to the nearest 2 mmHg.\n7. Take **2–3 readings 1–2 min apart** and average them. Measure **both arms** at the first visit; use the higher arm thereafter (a difference > 10–15 mmHg is significant).\n8. For postural BP: lying, then standing after 1 and 3 min. **Orthostatic hypotension** is a fall of ≥ 20 systolic / ≥ 10 diastolic.\n\n## Korotkoff phases\n- **I**: first clear tapping sound = **systolic BP**.\n- **II**: softer, swishing murmur.\n- **III**: crisper, louder tapping.\n- **IV**: **muffling** (≈ 5–10 mmHg above phase V).\n- **V**: **disappearance** = **diastolic BP** (adults).\n\n**Phase IV is used** for diastolic when sounds persist to zero: pregnancy, children, aortic regurgitation, thyrotoxicosis, anaemia, after exercise.\n\n**Auscultatory gap**: a silent interval between phases I and II, common in hypertensive and elderly people with stiff arteries. It can cause underestimation of systolic BP (or overestimation of diastolic). Avoided by the palpatory method.\n\n## Normal values (adults)\n- **Normal ≈ 120/80 mmHg**. Pulse pressure ≈ 40; mean arterial pressure ≈ diastolic + 1/3 pulse pressure (≈ 93).\n- **ISH/Indian guidelines**: normal < 130/85, **hypertension ≥ 140/90** (clinic). **ACC/AHA 2017**: stage 1 ≥ 130/80.\n- Indian prevalence ≈ 25–30% of adults (NFHS-5: ≈ 24% of men, 21% of women ≥ 15 yr).\n- **Physiological variations**: rises with age (systolic), exercise, emotion, meals, cold, pregnancy (falls in the 2nd trimester); lower in sleep; higher in men before menopause; **white-coat effect**.\n- Legs: the popliteal systolic is **10–20 mmHg higher** than the brachial.\n  - **Hill's sign** (> 20–60 mmHg difference) in aortic regurgitation.\n  - Lower BP in the legs suggests **coarctation of the aorta**.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Cuff too small** gives a falsely **high** BP; too large gives a falsely low reading. The commonest error is \"cuff hypertension\" in obese people.\n- **Arm below heart level** gives a falsely high reading (≈ 0.8 mmHg per cm); above heart level, low.\n- **Rapid deflation** underestimates systolic and overestimates diastolic.\n- Loose cuff (high), cuff over clothing.\n- **Missing the auscultatory gap.**\n- Not resting; talking; full bladder; caffeine/smoking.\n- Parallax; terminal-digit preference (rounding to 0 or 5).\n- Uncalibrated aneroid device; mercury not at zero; leaking tubes.\n- Pressing the stethoscope too firmly (diastolic too low).\n- **Observer bias**; **white-coat hypertension**. **Masked hypertension** is detected by ambulatory (ABPM) or home monitoring.\n- Arrhythmia (AF): average several readings.\n\n## Other methods\n- Oscillometric (automated, measures MAP)\n- Ultrasound Doppler (infants, shock)\n- **Direct intra-arterial** (ICU)\n- Ambulatory 24-h BP monitoring","q":{"b":["Korotkoff sounds – turbulent flow in a partly compressed artery.","Rest 5 min, arm at heart level.","Bladder length ≥ 80%, width 40% of arm circumference.","Palpatory method first – avoids missing the auscultatory gap.","Inflate 20–30 mmHg above palpatory systolic.","Deflate 2–3 mmHg/s.","Phase I = systolic; phase V = diastolic.","Phase IV in pregnancy, children, AR.","Small cuff → falsely high BP.","Arm below heart → falsely high.","Hypertension ≥ 140/90 (Indian/ESH).","Orthostatic hypotension: fall ≥ 20/10.","Leg systolic 10–20 mmHg higher than arm."],"n":["120/80 mmHg","HTN ≥ 140/90","Deflate 2–3 mmHg/s","Bladder 40% × 80%","Rest 5 min"],"m":"Phases **\"Tap, Swish, Thump, Muffle, Silence\"** – I to V.","v":["Effect of a small cuff? Falsely high BP.","Why use the palpatory method first? To avoid missing the auscultatory gap.","Diastolic in pregnancy? Phase IV (or V by current guidelines)."]}},"pr|Clinical examination of CVS":{"d":"## Principle\nA systematic bedside examination with the **patient's consent**, a **chaperone** where needed, and adequate exposure. The patient lies **reclining at 45°**. The examiner stands on the **right side**, in good light. **Sequence: inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation**, after the general examination.\n\n## General examination relevant to CVS\n- **Pallor, cyanosis** (central vs peripheral), **clubbing** (cyanotic congenital heart disease, infective endocarditis), oedema (pitting, pedal or sacral), **jaundice**.\n- Signs of **infective endocarditis**: splinter haemorrhages, Osler's nodes, Janeway lesions.\n- Xanthomas, malar flush (mitral stenosis), Marfanoid habitus.\n\n## Arterial pulse (radial)\nExamine for:\n- **rate** (60–100/min; count for a full minute if irregular)\n- **rhythm** (regular, regularly irregular, irregularly irregular as in AF with a **pulse deficit** > 10)\n- **volume**, **character** (best at the carotid: collapsing/water-hammer in AR; slow-rising/anacrotic in AS; pulsus paradoxus in tamponade; pulsus alternans in LV failure; bisferiens)\n- **condition of the vessel wall**\n- **radio-radial and radio-femoral delay** (coarctation)\n- all **peripheral pulses** compared bilaterally\n\n**BP** in both arms (see the BP chapter).\n\n## Jugular venous pressure (JVP)\n- Patient at 45°, head turned slightly left. Look at the **internal jugular vein** (between the heads of the sternocleidomastoid).\n- **JVP = vertical height above the sternal angle + 5 cm** (the sternal angle is ≈ 5 cm above the right atrium). **Normal ≤ 3–4 cm above the sternal angle (≤ 8–9 cmH₂O)**.\n- Venous vs arterial pulsation: the venous pulse is **double-peaked**, not palpable, **obliterated by pressure** at the root of the neck, and **varies with respiration and posture**.\n- **Hepatojugular reflux** (abdominojugular test) is positive in right heart failure.\n- **Waveform**: a (atrial contraction; **giant a** in pulmonary hypertension or tricuspid stenosis; **cannon a** in complete heart block; absent in AF), c, x descent, v (**large v** in tricuspid regurgitation), y descent.\n- **Kussmaul's sign** (JVP rises on inspiration): constrictive pericarditis.\n\n## Precordium\n### Inspection\nShape (bulge), **apex impulse**, visible pulsations, scars (sternotomy, mitral valvotomy: left thoracotomy), dilated veins.\n\n### Palpation\n- **Apex beat** is the **lowermost and outermost point of definite cardiac impulse**. **Normal in the left 5th intercostal space, ≈ 1 cm medial to the mid-clavicular line**.\n  - **Displaced** with LV enlargement.\n  - **Heaving** (sustained) in LV hypertrophy (pressure overload: AS, hypertension).\n  - **Hyperdynamic** in volume overload (MR, AR).\n  - **Tapping** (palpable S1) in mitral stenosis.\n- **Parasternal heave** (RV hypertrophy).\n- **Thrills** (palpable murmurs).\n- Palpable P2 (pulmonary hypertension).\n\n### Percussion\nCardiac dullness: largely replaced by echo, but still taught.\n\n## Auscultation\nUse the **diaphragm** (high pitch: S1, S2, regurgitant murmurs) and **bell** (low pitch: S3, S4, mid-diastolic murmur of mitral stenosis).\n\n### Areas\n- **Mitral**: apex.\n- **Tricuspid**: left lower sternal border, 4th–5th ICS.\n- **Pulmonary**: left 2nd ICS.\n- **Aortic**: right 2nd ICS.\n- **Second aortic (Erb's)**: left 3rd ICS (AR murmur).\n\n**Sounds**:\n- **S1** (\"lub\") is closure of the AV valves; best at the apex.\n- **S2** (\"dub\") is closure of the semilunar valves.\n  - **Physiological splitting** on inspiration (A2 then P2).\n  - **Wide fixed splitting** in ASD.\n  - **Paradoxical splitting** in LBBB or AS.\n- **S3**: rapid filling. Normal in children/athletes; ventricular failure in adults.\n- **S4**: atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle. Always abnormal.\n\n**Murmurs** are described by timing (systolic/diastolic), site, radiation (MR to the axilla, AS to the carotids), grade (Levine 1–6), pitch, and response to manoeuvres (respiration, posture: left lateral for MS, sitting forward in expiration for AR).\n\n**Rheumatic heart disease** is still common in India, so **mitral stenosis** murmurs are frequently seen.\n\n## Sources of error and precautions\n- Examining over clothing; a noisy room.\n- Not timing murmurs against the carotid pulse.\n- Missing the left-lateral position for MS and leaning forward for AR.\n- **Obesity, emphysema and pericardial effusion** muffle heart sounds and make the apex impalpable.\n- Confusing venous and arterial neck pulsations.\n- Dextrocardia: check the right side if the apex is not felt.\n- Hand hygiene before and after (see General examination).","q":{"b":["Patient at 45°; examiner on the right.","Order: inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation.","Pulse: rate, rhythm, volume, character, wall, delay.","Radio-femoral delay – coarctation.","JVP = height above sternal angle + 5 cm; normal ≤ 3–4 cm.","Venous pulse double-peaked, non-palpable, compressible.","Cannon a waves – complete heart block; no a wave in AF.","Apex beat: left 5th ICS, 1 cm medial to MCL.","Heaving apex – pressure overload; hyperdynamic – volume overload.","Areas: mitral apex, tricuspid LLSB, pulmonary L2, aortic R2.","Wide fixed split S2 – ASD.","S3 normal in the young; S4 always abnormal.","Bell for low-pitched sounds (MS murmur)."],"n":["Pulse 60–100/min","JVP ≤ 4 cm above sternal angle","Apex 5th ICS","Sternal angle 5 cm above RA"],"m":"Auscultation order **\"All People Enjoy Time Magazine\"** – Aortic, Pulmonary, Erb's, Tricuspid, Mitral.","v":["Normal position of the apex beat? Left 5th ICS, 1 cm medial to the midclavicular line.","Fixed splitting of S2? Atrial septal defect.","Cannon a waves? Complete heart block."]}},"pr|Clinical examination of respiratory system":{"d":"## Principle\nBedside assessment of the lungs and pleura using **inspection, palpation, percussion and auscultation**. Front and back are compared **side to side at corresponding points**. The patient sits upright, the chest is exposed, consent is taken, and a chaperone is present where appropriate.\n\n## History pointers\nCough, sputum (amount, colour, blood: **haemoptysis**, suspect TB in India), breathlessness (mMRC grade), wheeze, chest pain (pleuritic), fever, smoking (pack-years), biomass fuel exposure, occupation.\n\n## General examination\n- Respiratory rate (**normal adult 12–20/min**).\n- Use of **accessory muscles**, pursed-lip breathing.\n- **Cyanosis**, **clubbing** (bronchiectasis, lung abscess, empyema, carcinoma; **not** in uncomplicated COPD).\n- Flapping tremor (CO₂ retention), lymph nodes, SpO₂ (normal ≥ 95%).\n\n## Inspection\n- Shape of the chest. Normal **transverse : AP ratio ≈ 7:5**. **Barrel chest** (ratio near 1:1) in emphysema. Pectus excavatum/carinatum, kyphoscoliosis.\n- Breathing type. **Thoraco-abdominal in males, thoracic in females**.\n- Movement symmetry: **reduced on the side of pathology**.\n- Trachea position (seen at the suprasternal notch), apex, scars, intercostal indrawing.\n\n## Palpation\n- **Trachea**: central normally. **Pushed away** by pleural effusion or pneumothorax; **pulled towards** collapse or fibrosis.\n- **Apex beat**: confirms mediastinal shift.\n- **Chest expansion**: measured with a tape at the 4th ICS. **Normal ≈ 5 cm (range 3–8 cm)**, reduced in COPD and ankylosing spondylitis. Bilateral hand method for asymmetry.\n- **Vocal fremitus**: increased in consolidation; decreased in effusion, pneumothorax and collapse with blocked bronchus.\n\n## Percussion\nMiddle finger pressed flat, struck by the other middle finger; compare sides. Notes:\n- **Resonant**: normal lung.\n- **Hyper-resonant**: pneumothorax, emphysema.\n- **Impaired / dull**: consolidation, collapse, fibrosis.\n- **Stony dull**: pleural effusion.\n- **Tidal percussion**: the upper border of liver dullness (right 5th ICS in the MCL) moves down 2–3 cm on inspiration.\n- Normal cardiac and liver dullness are obliterated in emphysema.\n\n## Auscultation\n- Breath sounds: **vesicular** (normal; inspiration longer than expiration, no gap) vs **bronchial** (tubular, expiration ≥ inspiration with a gap; consolidation, cavity), and intensity.\n- **Vocal resonance**:\n  - increased in consolidation\n  - whispering pectoriloquy and **aegophony** over consolidation or at the upper level of an effusion\n  - reduced in effusion or pneumothorax\n- **Added sounds**:\n  - **Wheeze** (rhonchi): musical, narrowed airways; asthma, COPD.\n  - **Crackles** (crepitations): **fine late-inspiratory** in pulmonary oedema or fibrosis; **coarse** in bronchiectasis.\n  - **Pleural rub**: pleurisy.\n\n## Classic patterns\n- **Consolidation**: dull, increased fremitus, bronchial breathing, increased vocal resonance.\n- **Effusion**: stony dull, reduced fremitus, reduced breath sounds, trachea shifted away.\n- **Pneumothorax**: hyper-resonant, absent breath sounds, shifted away.\n- **Collapse**: dull, shifted towards, reduced sounds.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Not comparing symmetrical points.\n- Percussing too hard (deep structures interfere) or over the scapula.\n- Patient talking or shivering (muscle sounds).\n- Stethoscope hair-rub mimicking crackles.\n- Tracheal deviation from goitre or kyphoscoliosis.\n- Obesity reduces signs.","q":{"b":["Respiratory rate 12–20/min adult.","Chest ratio transverse:AP ≈ 7:5; barrel chest ≈ 1:1.","Breathing thoraco-abdominal in men, thoracic in women.","Expansion ≈ 5 cm at 4th ICS.","Trachea pushed by effusion/pneumothorax; pulled by collapse/fibrosis.","Fremitus ↑ consolidation, ↓ effusion.","Stony dull = effusion; hyper-resonant = pneumothorax.","Bronchial breathing = consolidation.","Fine crackles – oedema/fibrosis; wheeze – airway narrowing.","Clubbing not due to uncomplicated COPD.","Compare side-to-side always."],"n":["RR 12–20/min","SpO₂ ≥ 95%","Expansion 3–8 cm (≈5)","Tidal percussion 2–3 cm"],"m":"**\"I Pay Particular Attention\"** – Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation.","v":["Stony dullness indicates? Pleural effusion.","Bronchial breath sounds are heard in? Consolidation (also cavity).","Trachea shifted towards the lesion? Collapse or fibrosis."]}},"pr|Clinical examination of abdomen and nervous system":{"d":"## Abdomen: principle and position\nThe patient lies **supine, flat, with one pillow, arms by the sides, knees slightly flexed** (to relax the abdominal wall), bladder emptied. Expose from **nipples to mid-thigh** with dignity; chaperone. Examiner on the right; **warm hands**. Ask about pain and **examine the painful area last**.\n\n## Abdomen: inspection\n- Shape: scaphoid, distended. The **5 Fs**: fat, fluid, flatus, faeces, foetus.\n- Umbilicus (normally inverted; everted in ascites).\n- Movement with respiration.\n- Visible peristalsis (obstruction), pulsations, dilated veins (direction of flow), scars, hernial orifices (ask to cough).\n\n## Abdomen: palpation\n- **Superficial** palpation for tenderness, guarding and rigidity, then **deep** palpation in all **nine regions**.\n- **Liver**: start from the right iliac fossa and move up with inspiration. Normally just palpable in thin people or children; **span by percussion ≈ 12–15 cm in the MCL** (sources give 10–15 cm).\n- **Spleen**: from the right iliac fossa **towards the left costal margin** (it enlarges towards the RIF). It is palpable only when enlarged (≈ 2–3 times normal). It has a **notch**, you **cannot get above it**, it moves with respiration, and it is dull to percussion. Splenomegaly is common in India: malaria, kala-azar, portal hypertension.\n- **Kidneys**: **bimanually ballotable**; resonant band of colon in front.\n\n## Abdomen: percussion and auscultation\n- Liver span; **shifting dullness** (needs > ≈ 500 ml of ascites); **fluid thrill** (gross ascites).\n- **Bowel sounds** every 5–10 s. Absent in ileus (listen ≥ 2 min), exaggerated in obstruction. Renal bruit.\n- Complete with hernial orifices, external genitalia, and per-rectal examination when indicated.\n\n## Nervous system: principle\nLocalise the lesion, from cortex to muscle, by examining:\n1. **higher functions**\n2. **cranial nerves** (separate chapter)\n3. **motor system**\n4. **sensory system**\n5. **reflexes**\n6. **coordination**\n7. **gait**\n8. signs of meningeal irritation\n\n## Higher functions\nConsciousness (**GCS 3–15**: Eye 4, Verbal 5, Motor 6), orientation, memory, speech (dysarthria vs aphasia), handedness, MMSE (30 points).\n\n## Motor system\n- **Bulk**: wasting, fasciculations.\n- **Tone**:\n  - **spasticity** (clasp-knife) in UMN lesions\n  - **rigidity** (lead-pipe or cogwheel) in extrapyramidal disease\n  - hypotonia in LMN or cerebellar lesions\n- **Power**: **MRC grade 0–5**.\n  - 0 no contraction\n  - 1 flicker\n  - 2 movement with gravity eliminated\n  - 3 against gravity\n  - 4 against some resistance\n  - 5 normal\n- **UMN vs LMN**: UMN gives **no wasting, spasticity, brisk reflexes and an extensor plantar**. LMN gives **wasting, fasciculations, hypotonia, absent reflexes and a flexor or absent plantar**.\n\n## Sensory system\n- **Spinothalamic**: pain (pin), temperature.\n- **Dorsal column**: fine touch, **vibration (128 Hz fork)**, joint position, **Romberg test** (positive in sensory ataxia, not in cerebellar ataxia).\n- Map against **dermatomes**: **C6 thumb, C7 middle finger, C8 little finger, T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L4 medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot**.\n\n## Coordination\n- **Cerebellar signs**: finger-nose test (intention tremor, past-pointing), heel-shin test, **dysdiadochokinesia**, nystagmus, scanning speech.\n- **Gait**: spastic (circumduction), ataxic (broad-based), Parkinsonian (shuffling, festinant), high-stepping (foot drop).\n- **Meningeal signs**: neck stiffness, **Kernig's** and **Brudzinski's** signs.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Abdomen: cold hands, tense patient, palpating from the wrong direction (missing a large spleen), full bladder mistaken for a mass.\n- Neuro:\n  - uncooperative patient\n  - comparing non-homologous sites\n  - testing sensation with the patient looking\n  - suggestion bias (\"is this sharp?\")\n  - tuning fork not placed on bone\n  - anxiety increasing reflexes or tone","q":{"b":["Abdomen: supine, one pillow, knees flexed, warm hands, painful area last.","Nine regions; superficial then deep palpation.","Liver/spleen palpated from RIF upward with inspiration.","Spleen: notch, can't get above, moves with respiration.","Kidney ballotable; colonic resonance in front.","Shifting dullness needs ≈ 500 ml fluid.","Bowel sounds – listen ≥ 2 min before calling absent.","Neuro order: higher functions, CN, motor, sensory, reflexes, coordination, gait.","GCS 3–15 (E4 V5 M6).","MRC power 0–5; grade 3 = against gravity.","UMN: spastic, brisk, Babinski; LMN: wasting, flaccid, areflexic.","Romberg positive = sensory (dorsal column) ataxia.","T10 dermatome = umbilicus."],"n":["Liver span 12–15 cm","GCS 3–15","MRC 0–5","Vibration fork 128 Hz","Ascites detectable ≈ 500 ml"],"m":"Abdominal distension **5 Fs** – Fat, Fluid, Flatus, Faeces, Foetus.","v":["Romberg's sign tests? Proprioception (dorsal columns).","Direction of splenic enlargement? Towards the right iliac fossa.","Clasp-knife spasticity indicates? Upper motor neuron lesion."]}},"pr|Reflexes":{"d":"## Principle\nA reflex is an **involuntary, stereotyped response to a stimulus**. It is mediated by a **reflex arc**: receptor, afferent, centre, efferent, effector.\n\n### Deep tendon (stretch) reflexes\n- Tapping a tendon stretches the **muscle spindle**, exciting **Ia afferents**.\n- These synapse **monosynaptically** on **α-motor neurons**, causing contraction of the same muscle.\n- They test the integrity of a particular **spinal segment** and the **descending (UMN) control**.\n\n### Superficial reflexes\nThese are **polysynaptic** responses to skin stimulation (abdominal, plantar, cremasteric).\n\n## Procedure\n- Patient relaxed and comfortable, limbs symmetrical. Use a **knee hammer** held loosely, swung from the wrist.\n- Strike the tendon briskly, observe and palpate the contraction, and **compare both sides**.\n- If absent, use **reinforcement**:\n  - **Jendrassik's manoeuvre**: clench the teeth or pull hooked fingers apart while the leg is tapped.\n  - Reinforcement increases **γ-motor (fusimotor) drive** and α excitability.\n\n## Deep reflexes (segment)\n- **Biceps (C5, C6)**: tap your thumb placed on the biceps tendon; elbow flexes.\n- **Supinator / brachioradialis (C5, C6)**: tap the lower radius; elbow flexes.\n- **Triceps (C6, C7)**: tap the tendon above the olecranon with the arm flexed; elbow extends.\n- **Knee jerk (L2, L3, L4; mainly L3–L4)**: tap the patellar tendon with the knees flexed and supported; knee extends.\n- **Ankle jerk (S1, S2)**: tap the Achilles tendon with the foot slightly dorsiflexed; plantar flexion.\n- **Jaw jerk (trigeminal, pons)**: brisk in bilateral UMN lesions above the pons (pseudobulbar palsy).\n\n## Grading (NINDS)\n- **0** absent\n- **1+** diminished (trace)\n- **2+** normal\n- **3+** brisk / increased\n- **4+** with **clonus**\n\n**Clonus** is rhythmic contractions on sustained stretch (ankle, patella). **Sustained clonus (> 3–5 beats)** indicates a UMN lesion.\n\n## Superficial reflexes\n- **Abdominal reflexes**: upper **T7–T9**, lower **T11–T12**. Absent in UMN lesions, obesity, multiparous women, and after surgery.\n- **Cremasteric (L1, L2)**.\n- **Plantar (L5, S1, S2)**: stroke the lateral sole from heel to little toe, curving medially, with a blunt object.\n  - **Normal adult**: **flexor** (toes curl down).\n  - **Babinski sign**: **extensor** (big toe dorsiflexion with fanning of the other toes). Indicates a **pyramidal tract lesion**.\n  - **Normal in infants**, until myelination of the corticospinal tracts at about **1–2 years** (sources vary, 12–24 months).\n\n## Interpretation\n- **Hyperreflexia**, clonus and an extensor plantar: **UMN lesion** (loss of descending inhibition).\n- **Hyporeflexia / areflexia**: LMN, peripheral neuropathy, root, muscle disease, **spinal shock** (acute phase).\n- **Pendular knee jerk**: **cerebellar disease**.\n- **Hung-up (slow-relaxing) ankle jerk**: **hypothyroidism**.\n- **Inverted supinator jerk**: C5–C6 lesion.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Tense patient, so the reflex is suppressed (use reinforcement).\n- Hammer held too tightly; striking muscle instead of tendon.\n- Asymmetric limb positions.\n- Anxiety exaggerating responses.\n- Plantar: a too-sharp stimulus gives withdrawal; callused sole.\n- Clonus confused with tremor.\n- Elderly people may lose the ankle jerk physiologically.","q":{"b":["Stretch reflex: spindle → Ia → α-motor neuron (monosynaptic).","Biceps C5–6, supinator C5–6, triceps C6–7.","Knee L3–4 (L2–4), ankle S1–2.","Grades 0 to 4+; 2+ normal; 4+ with clonus.","Jendrassik reinforcement increases γ-drive.","Abdominal T7–12; cremasteric L1–2.","Plantar L5–S2; adult flexor.","Babinski = pyramidal lesion; normal in infants up to ~1–2 y.","UMN: brisk reflexes, clonus, extensor plantar.","Pendular jerk – cerebellar; hung-up ankle – hypothyroidism.","Spinal shock: areflexia initially."],"n":["Knee L3–L4","Ankle S1","Biceps C5–6","Triceps C7","Sustained clonus > 3–5 beats"],"m":"Reflex roots: **\"1-2 buckle my shoe (ankle S1-2), 3-4 kick the door (knee L3-4), 5-6 pick up sticks (biceps C5-6), 7-8 shut the gate (triceps C7-8)\"**.","v":["Root of the ankle jerk? S1 (S1–S2).","Significance of an extensor plantar response? Pyramidal (UMN) tract lesion.","Pendular knee jerk is seen in? Cerebellar disease."]}},"pr|ECG recording":{"d":"## Principle\nThe **electrocardiogram** records, from the body surface, the **sum of electrical potential changes** of the myocardium during depolarisation and repolarisation. Body fluids act as a **volume conductor**.\n\n- A wave of depolarisation **moving towards** a positive electrode gives an upward deflection.\n- **Einthoven's triangle**: the limb leads I, II and III form an equilateral triangle around the heart.\n- **Einthoven's law: II = I + III**.\n\n## Apparatus and leads\n- ECG machine with electrodes and conducting gel.\n- **12 leads**:\n  - **Standard bipolar limb leads**:\n    - **I** (LA+, RA−)\n    - **II** (LL+, RA−)\n    - **III** (LL+, LA−)\n  - **Augmented unipolar leads**: aVR, aVL, aVF (Goldberger).\n  - **Chest (precordial) leads**:\n    - **V1** right 4th ICS, parasternal\n    - **V2** left 4th ICS, parasternal\n    - **V3** midway between V2 and V4\n    - **V4** left 5th ICS, MCL\n    - **V5** anterior axillary line, same level\n    - **V6** mid-axillary line, same level\n- Limb electrodes are colour-coded:\n  - **RA red, LA yellow, LL green, RL black** (ground); IEC scheme\n  - mnemonic \"Ride Your Green Bike\"\n\n## Procedure\n1. Explain, take consent, ensure privacy. The patient lies **supine and relaxed**, warm, with metal removed.\n2. Clean the skin and apply gel. Place the limb and chest electrodes.\n3. **Standardisation**: **1 mV = 10 mm** deflection. **Paper speed 25 mm/s**.\n4. Record all 12 leads, plus a long **lead II rhythm strip**.\n5. Label with name, age, date and time.\n\n## Paper calibration\n- **Small square = 1 mm = 0.04 s**.\n- **Large square = 5 mm = 0.2 s**.\n- **Heart rate**:\n  - **300 ÷ number of large squares** between R waves, or **1500 ÷ small squares**.\n  - If irregular: number of QRS in 6 s (30 large squares) × 10.\n\n## Normal values (adult)\n- **P wave**: atrial depolarisation. Duration **≤ 0.11 s (< 0.12)**, amplitude **≤ 2.5 mm**. Upright in I, II and aVF; inverted in aVR.\n- **PR interval**: **0.12–0.20 s**. Reflects AV nodal delay.\n- **QRS**: ventricular depolarisation, **0.06–0.10 s (< 0.12)**.\n- **Q wave**: normal if < 0.04 s and < 25% of R.\n- **ST segment**: isoelectric (plateau).\n- **T wave**: ventricular repolarisation. Same direction as QRS because repolarisation proceeds epicardium to endocardium.\n- **QT interval**: ≈ **0.35–0.43 s**, rate-dependent. **QTc (Bazett) = QT/√RR**; normal **≤ 0.44 s** (sources: < 0.45 men, < 0.46–0.47 women).\n- **U wave**: occasional; prominent in hypokalaemia.\n- **Mean QRS axis**: **−30° to +90°** (Guyton: ≈ +59°; some texts −30 to +110).\n  - **Left axis deviation** < −30°.\n  - **Right axis deviation** > +90°.\n  - Determine from leads I and aVF (both positive = normal).\n\n## Interpretation outline\nRate, rhythm (sinus: P before every QRS), axis, intervals, P morphology, QRS (hypertrophy: Sokolow–Lyon SV1 + RV5/6 > 35 mm suggests LVH), ST/T changes (ischaemia, infarction), and electrolyte effects:\n- **Hyperkalaemia**: tall tented T.\n- **Hypokalaemia**: U wave, flat T.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Muscle tremor / shivering**: irregular baseline.\n- **AC interference** (50 Hz in India): regular fine oscillations; check grounding.\n- **Wandering baseline**: breathing, poor contact.\n- **Lead reversal**: RA/LA swap inverts lead I and makes aVR upright.\n- Wrong chest-lead placement mimics infarction or poor R progression.\n- Incorrect calibration or paper speed.\n- Dry or hairy skin.","q":{"b":["ECG = surface record of summed cardiac potentials; volume conductor.","Einthoven's law: II = I + III.","12 leads: 3 bipolar limb, 3 augmented, 6 chest.","V1/V2 4th ICS parasternal; V4 5th ICS MCL.","Standard 10 mm/mV at 25 mm/s.","Small square 0.04 s; large square 0.2 s.","HR = 300/large squares = 1500/small squares.","PR 0.12–0.20 s; QRS < 0.12 s; P < 0.12 s.","QTc ≤ 0.44 s (Bazett QT/√RR).","Normal axis −30° to +90°.","T wave upright because repolarisation epi → endo.","50 Hz interference – check earthing.","RA/LA reversal → inverted lead I."],"n":["PR 0.12–0.20 s","QRS 0.06–0.10 s","QT 0.35–0.43 s","1 mV = 10 mm","25 mm/s"],"m":"Limb electrodes **\"Ride Your Green Bike\"** – Red RA, Yellow LA, Green LL, Black RL.","v":["What does the PR interval represent? Conduction from atrial depolarisation through the AV node to ventricular depolarisation (AV delay).","Einthoven's law? Lead II = Lead I + Lead III.","Why is the T wave upright? Repolarisation proceeds from epicardium to endocardium, opposite to depolarisation."]}},"pr|Spirometry":{"d":"## Principle\nSpirometry measures the **volume of air inspired and expired** over time.\n\n- **Recording spirometer**: a bell floating in water, writing on a kymograph.\n- **Modern electronic spirometers**: pneumotachograph or turbine flow sensors, with volume obtained by integrating flow.\n\nIt measures static lung volumes (except **residual volume**) and **dynamic, timed volumes** (FEV₁, FVC). It cannot measure **RV, FRC or TLC**; these need **helium dilution, nitrogen washout or body plethysmography**.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Explain and demonstrate. Record **age, sex, height and weight**: predicted values depend on these (Indian reference equations, e.g. GLI or ethnic correction).\n2. The subject sits upright with a **nose clip**, lips sealed around the mouthpiece.\n3. **Tidal breathing**, then a maximal inspiration to TLC, then a **forced, rapid, complete expiration** for **≥ 6 s** (FVC manoeuvre). Encourage vigorously.\n4. **Slow vital capacity** and other volumes from quiet breathing.\n5. Perform **at least 3 acceptable manoeuvres**. **Repeatability**: the two best FVC and FEV₁ within **150 ml** (ATS/ERS).\n6. Report values at **BTPS** (body temperature, pressure, saturated).\n7. **Bronchodilator reversibility**: repeat 15 min after 400 µg salbutamol. **≥ 12% and ≥ 200 ml rise in FEV₁** is significant (asthma).\n\n## Normal values (young adult male; females ≈ 20–25% lower)\n- **Tidal volume (TV)**: ≈ **500 ml**.\n- **Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV)**: ≈ **3000 ml** (sources give 2500–3300).\n- **Expiratory reserve volume (ERV)**: ≈ **1100–1200 ml**.\n- **Residual volume (RV)**: ≈ **1200 ml**.\n- **Inspiratory capacity (IC = TV + IRV)**: ≈ 3500 ml.\n- **FRC (ERV + RV)**: ≈ **2300–2400 ml**.\n- **Vital capacity (VC = IRV + TV + ERV)**: ≈ **4.6–4.8 L** (Indian adults are often lower, ≈ 3.5–4.5 L).\n- **TLC**: ≈ **5.8–6 L**.\n- **FEV₁/FVC**: **≈ 80%** (> 70% normal; it falls with age).\n- **FEV₁** ≈ 80% of FVC; FEV₂ ≈ 94%; FEV₃ ≈ 97%.\n- **Maximum voluntary ventilation (MVV / MBC)**: ≈ **120–170 L/min**. Breathing reserve = (MVV − minute ventilation)/MVV, ≈ 95%.\n- **Minute ventilation** = TV × RR ≈ **6 L/min**.\n- **PEFR** (see separate chapter) ≈ 400–600 L/min.\n- **FEF 25–75%** reflects small airways.\n\n## Interpretation\n- **Obstructive** (asthma, COPD):\n  - **FEV₁/FVC < 0.70** (or below the lower limit of normal)\n  - FEV₁ reduced; FVC normal or reduced\n  - \"scooped\" flow–volume loop\n  - **COPD (GOLD)** is **post-bronchodilator FEV₁/FVC < 0.70**\n- **Restrictive** (fibrosis, kyphoscoliosis, obesity, neuromuscular disease): **FVC reduced, FEV₁/FVC normal or raised**. Confirm low TLC.\n- **Flow–volume loop**: fixed upper-airway obstruction flattens both limbs.\n\n## Factors affecting VC\n- Sex, age, height and posture: lower when supine.\n- Training: higher in swimmers and athletes.\n- Pregnancy, ascites, lung disease.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Poor effort, a submaximal start (back-extrapolated volume > 5% or 150 ml).\n- **Leak** at the lips or no nose clip.\n- **Early termination** (< 6 s) underestimates FVC.\n- Cough during the first second; hesitation.\n- Uncalibrated device: calibrate daily with a **3 L syringe**.\n- No BTPS correction (old water spirometers).\n- Wrong height entry.\n- Using Caucasian predicted equations for Indians overestimates \"abnormality\".\n- Contraindications: recent MI, pneumothorax, eye/abdominal surgery, haemoptysis.","q":{"b":["Spirometer cannot measure RV, FRC, TLC.","Use helium dilution / N₂ washout / plethysmography for RV.","TV 500, IRV 3000, ERV 1100, RV 1200 ml.","VC ≈ 4.6 L; TLC ≈ 6 L; FRC ≈ 2.3 L.","FEV₁/FVC ≈ 80%; < 70% = obstruction.","Restriction: ↓FVC, normal/↑ ratio.","≥ 3 acceptable blows; repeatability within 150 ml.","Expire ≥ 6 s.","Reversibility: ≥ 12% and ≥ 200 ml FEV₁ rise.","Report at BTPS.","Calibrate with 3 L syringe.","MVV ≈ 120–170 L/min."],"n":["TV 500 ml","VC ≈ 4.6 L","TLC ≈ 6 L","FEV₁/FVC ≈ 80%","Repeatability 150 ml"],"m":"Capacities = sums: **\"VC = I-T-E\"** (IRV + TV + ERV); **TLC = VC + RV**.","v":["Volume not measurable by simple spirometry? Residual volume (hence FRC and TLC).","FEV₁/FVC in obstructive disease? Reduced, < 0.70.","Criterion for bronchodilator reversibility? FEV₁ rise ≥ 12% and ≥ 200 ml."]}},"pr|Visual acuity & perimetry":{"d":"## Visual acuity: principle\nVisual acuity is the **ability to discriminate two points as separate**. It is a function of **foveal cones** (the minimum separable). The normal eye resolves **1 minute of arc**.\n\nThe **Snellen chart** letters subtend **5 minutes of arc** at their designated distance, and each limb subtends 1 minute.\n\n## Distant vision procedure (Snellen)\n- Subject at **6 m (20 ft)**: rays are practically parallel, so accommodation is relaxed. Chart well illuminated.\n- Test **each eye separately**, the other covered without pressure, first without and then with glasses.\n- Read from the top down. **VA = d/D**:\n  - **d** = test distance\n  - **D** = distance at which the smallest read line should be read\n  - **Normal 6/6 (20/20)**; 6/5 or 6/4 possible\n  - The top letter is read at 60 m (**6/60**)\n- If < 6/60: move the subject closer (5/60 … 1/60), then **counting fingers**, **hand movements**, **perception of light** with projection, and **no PL**.\n- **Pinhole**: improvement means a **refractive error**.\n- **LogMAR charts** are used in research; **E-chart / Landolt C** for illiterates and children.\n- **Near vision**: **Jaeger chart** or N-notation at **≈ 33–40 cm**; normal **N6 / J1**.\n- WHO (ICD-11) categories:\n  - **Blindness**: presenting VA < 3/60 in the better eye.\n  - **Moderate–severe impairment**: < 6/18.\n  - India's NPCBVI uses **< 3/60** for blindness (revised 2017 from 6/60).\n\n## Perimetry: principle\nThe **visual field** is the area seen with one eye while fixating a point.\n\n**Normal monocular extent** (sources vary by a few degrees):\n- **temporal ≈ 90–100°**\n- **nasal ≈ 60°**\n- **superior ≈ 50–60°**\n- **inferior ≈ 70°**\n\n**Binocular field ≈ 180–200°.**\n\nThe **physiological blind spot** (optic disc) lies **≈ 12–15° temporal to fixation, just below the horizontal**.\n\n## Procedure\n- **Confrontation test** (bedside):\n  - examiner and subject face each other at about 1 m, each covering the opposite eye\n  - compare fields using a moving finger or red pin brought in from the periphery\n  - assumes the examiner's field is normal\n- **Lister's perimeter**:\n  - an arc with the chin rest at the centre, eye fixating the central point\n  - a white (or coloured) target is moved from the periphery inwards along meridians at 30° intervals\n  - mark the point where it is first seen\n- **Automated static perimetry** (Humphrey) in clinics; **Bjerrum (tangent) screen** for central fields.\n- **Colour fields**: **white widest, then blue, red, and green narrowest** (rods and cones are distributed differently).\n\n## Field defects (lesion localisation)\n- Optic nerve: monocular blindness.\n- **Optic chiasma** (pituitary tumour): **bitemporal hemianopia**.\n- **Optic tract**: incongruous **contralateral homonymous hemianopia**.\n- **Meyer's loop** (temporal lobe): **superior quadrantanopia** (\"pie in the sky\").\n- Parietal radiation: inferior quadrantanopia.\n- **Occipital cortex**: homonymous hemianopia with **macular sparing** (dual blood supply).\n- **Glaucoma**: arcuate scotoma and peripheral constriction.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Inadequate illumination or a wrong distance.\n- Memorised chart; peeking or pressing on the covered eye.\n- Uncorrected refractive error.\n- Fatigue and poor fixation during perimetry; target moved too fast.\n- Nose, brows and ptosis restrict the field.\n- Small pupils.\n- Confrontation is crude: it detects only gross defects.","q":{"b":["Normal eye resolves 1 minute of arc.","Snellen letter subtends 5′ at its distance.","Test at 6 m – accommodation relaxed.","VA = d/D; normal 6/6.","Pinhole improvement → refractive error.","Near vision at 33–40 cm: N6/J1.","Field: temporal 90–100°, nasal 60°, sup 50–60°, inf 70°.","Blind spot 12–15° temporal to fixation.","Colour fields: white > blue > red > green.","Chiasmal lesion → bitemporal hemianopia.","Meyer's loop → superior quadrantanopia.","Occipital lesion → macular sparing."],"n":["VA 6/6","Snellen distance 6 m","Blind spot 15° temporal","Temporal field 90°+","Blindness < 3/60"],"m":"Colour field order **\"White Bears Rarely Growl\"** – White > Blue > Red > Green.","v":["Why is Snellen tested at 6 m? Rays are nearly parallel, so accommodation is relaxed.","Visual field defect in pituitary tumour? Bitemporal hemianopia.","Which colour has the smallest field? Green."]}},"pr|Hearing tests":{"d":"## Principle\nSound reaches the cochlea in two ways:\n- **Air conduction (AC)**: external ear, middle-ear ossicles, oval window. The normal, efficient route, thanks to the **impedance matching** of the middle ear.\n- **Bone conduction (BC)**: skull vibration directly stimulates the cochlea.\n\nTuning-fork tests compare AC with BC and the two ears with each other, to distinguish **conductive** (external/middle ear) from **sensorineural** (cochlea/VIII nerve) deafness.\n\n## Tuning fork\n- Use **512 Hz** (best balance: little vibrotactile sensation, slow decay).\n- 256 Hz may be felt rather than heard; 1024 Hz decays quickly.\n- Strike it on a **rubber pad or the elbow/knee**, not a hard surface (avoids overtones).\n\n## Rinne test\n- Place the vibrating fork on the **mastoid process** (BC). When it is no longer heard, hold the prongs **≈ 2.5 cm from the external meatus**, parallel to it (AC).\n- **Rinne positive (AC > BC)**: normal. Also in **sensorineural** loss, where both are reduced but AC is still > BC.\n- **Rinne negative (BC > AC)**: **conductive deafness** (usually ≥ 15–25 dB air–bone gap).\n- **False negative Rinne**: severe unilateral sensorineural deafness. The sound is heard in the opposite cochlea through bone; avoid by **masking** the good ear (Bárány noise box).\n\n## Weber test\n- Place the fork on the **vertex or forehead midline** (or upper incisors).\n- **Normal**: heard equally in both ears (centrally).\n- **Conductive deafness**: **lateralised to the affected (worse) ear**. Masking of ambient noise is lost, and the round window is less damped.\n- **Sensorineural deafness**: **lateralised to the better (normal) ear**.\n\n## Absolute bone conduction (ABC) test\n- Compare the patient's BC with the examiner's (normal) BC, with the meatus occluded.\n- **Normal in conductive loss; reduced in sensorineural loss**.\n- **Schwabach test**: similar, without occlusion. Prolonged in conductive loss, shortened in SNHL.\n\n## Simple bedside tests\n- **Whisper / free-field voice test**: the normal ear hears a conversational voice at 6 m (whisper at about 6 m in a quiet room); mask the other ear by rubbing the tragus.\n- **Watch tick test** (crude).\n\n## Audiometry (principle)\n- **Pure-tone audiometry**: thresholds in **dB HL** from **250 to 8000 Hz**, for AC and BC.\n- **Normal hearing threshold ≤ 20–25 dB HL** (sources vary).\n- **Air–bone gap** indicates conductive loss.\n- **Noise-induced hearing loss**: **notch at 4000 Hz** (common in Indian industrial workers).\n- **Presbycusis**: high-frequency loss.\n- **Impedance audiometry** (tympanometry): middle-ear pressure. **Type B (flat)** in effusion.\n- **BERA**: objective hearing; used for newborn screening.\n\n## Normal physiology numbers\n- Audible range **20–20,000 Hz**.\n- Most sensitive at **1000–4000 Hz**.\n- Speech range 500–2000 (up to 4000) Hz.\n- Threshold of pain ≈ **120–140 dB**.\n- Prolonged exposure > **85–90 dB** is damaging (occupational limit ≈ 90 dB for 8 h in India).\n\n## Sources of error\n- Fork struck too hard, producing overtones and tactile vibration.\n- Wrong fork frequency.\n- Fork placed off the mastoid or off the midline.\n- Prongs not parallel to the meatus.\n- No masking in unilateral severe loss, giving a false-negative Rinne.\n- Noisy room.\n- Wax impaction mimicking middle-ear disease.\n- Patient suggestion: ask \"where do you hear it?\" rather than \"is it on the left?\".","q":{"b":["Use 512 Hz fork.","Rinne: mastoid (BC) vs meatus (AC).","Rinne positive (AC > BC) – normal or SNHL.","Rinne negative – conductive loss.","False-negative Rinne in severe unilateral SNHL – mask.","Weber lateralises to affected ear in conductive loss.","Weber lateralises to better ear in SNHL.","ABC reduced in SNHL, normal in conductive.","Audible range 20–20,000 Hz; best 1–4 kHz.","Normal PTA threshold ≤ 20–25 dB.","NIHL notch at 4 kHz.","Damaging noise > 85–90 dB."],"n":["Fork 512 Hz","Range 20–20 kHz","Normal ≤ 25 dB HL","Pain 120–140 dB","NIHL 4 kHz notch"],"m":"**\"Weber: Conductive goes to the Crook, Sensorineural to the Sound ear\"**.","v":["Weber in right conductive deafness? Lateralised to the right.","Why use a 512 Hz fork? Good balance of decay time and little vibrotactile sensation.","Rinne result in normal ears? Positive (AC > BC)."]}},"pr|Ergography & human experiments":{"d":"## Principle\n**Ergography** records the **work done by a group of muscles** under repeated contractions, and the onset of **fatigue**.\n\n**Mosso's ergograph** has:\n- the subject's forearm fixed\n- the **middle finger** (flexor digitorum) pulling a weight (≈ **1–3 kg**) over a pulley **in rhythm with a metronome** (≈ 1 contraction per 2 s)\n- the excursions written on a kymograph (or a digital transducer)\n\n**Work done = load × total distance lifted (sum of heights)**, expressed in kg·m or J (1 kg·m ≈ 9.8 J).\n\n## Procedure\n1. The subject is seated. The forearm is strapped, palm up, with the other fingers fixed. A loop over the middle finger is connected to the load.\n2. Contract maximally to the beat until the finger can no longer lift the weight (**fatigue**).\n3. Calculate total work.\n4. Variations:\n   - rest\n   - massage\n   - occluding blood flow with a **BP cuff inflated above systolic** (fatigue occurs much faster: **ischaemia**)\n   - increased frequency\n   - mental distraction\n\n## Observations and physiology of fatigue\n- The height of contraction **declines progressively**.\n- In intact humans the **site of fatigue** is mainly:\n  - **within the muscle**: depletion of **phosphocreatine/glycogen**; accumulation of **Pi, H⁺** and lactate; impaired Ca²⁺ release\n  - plus a **central component**: motivation, **CNS fatigue**\n- Classic teaching (frog experiments) puts the first site of fatigue at the **neuromuscular junction** in nerve–muscle preparations, while the **nerve is practically indefatigable**.\n- **Recovery** after rest. Faster with massage and restored circulation (removal of metabolites, O₂ supply).\n- Encouragement or competition prolongs work: evidence for central fatigue.\n\n## Hand-grip dynamometry\n- Maximum voluntary grip strength: adult male **≈ 35–50 kg**, female **≈ 20–30 kg** (Indian values somewhat lower).\n- **Isometric handgrip at 30% MVC for 2–3 min** is an autonomic function test. A **diastolic rise > 15 mmHg is normal**; < 10 mmHg suggests sympathetic dysfunction.\n\n## Other human experiments in the CBME practical list\n- **Harvard step test** (physical fitness index):\n  - step 50.8 cm (20 in) for men, 30 times/min, 5 min\n  - **PFI = duration (s) × 100 / (2 × sum of 3 recovery pulse counts)**\n  - **< 55 poor, 55–64 low average, 65–79 high average, 80–89 good, > 90 excellent**\n- **Effect of exercise on HR, BP and respiration**: HR and systolic BP rise; diastolic changes little or falls (dynamic exercise).\n- **Cold pressor test**: hand in 4 °C water for 1 min. Systolic rises ≈ 10–20 mmHg normally; **hyper-reactors > 20–25 mmHg**.\n- **Autonomic tests**:\n  - **Valsalva ratio > 1.21** normal (≥ 1.45 in young people)\n  - **deep breathing HR difference > 15 bpm**\n  - **30:15 ratio > 1.04**\n  - **postural fall in systolic < 10 mmHg normal; ≥ 20 abnormal** (orthostatic hypotension)\n- **Mosso's ergograph and Treadmill / VO₂max**: Indian sedentary young males ≈ 35–45 ml/kg/min.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Load inappropriate for the subject.\n- Rhythm not maintained.\n- Other fingers helping (inadequate fixation).\n- Sub-maximal effort or motivation.\n- Kymograph speed errors.\n- Cuff deflating.\n- For the step test: wrong step height or cadence, and pulse counts mistimed.","q":{"b":["Mosso's ergograph: middle finger lifts 1–3 kg to a metronome.","Work = load × total distance.","Fatigue: progressive fall in height.","Fatigue faster with ischaemia (cuff above SBP).","Recovery hastened by rest and massage.","Nerve practically indefatigable; NMJ first site in frog prep.","In humans, fatigue is muscle + central components.","Grip: men 35–50 kg, women 20–30 kg.","Sustained handgrip: DBP rise > 15 mmHg normal.","Harvard PFI = time × 100 / (2 × sum of recovery pulses).","PFI > 90 excellent; < 55 poor.","Postural SBP fall ≥ 20 mmHg = orthostatic hypotension."],"n":["Ergograph load 1–3 kg","Step 50.8 cm, 30/min, 5 min","Handgrip DBP ↑ > 15","Valsalva ratio > 1.21","1 kg·m = 9.8 J"],"m":"Fatigue sites, frog prep **\"Nerve Never, Junction First, Muscle Next\"**.","v":["Which muscle does Mosso's ergograph use? Flexors of the middle finger (flexor digitorum).","Effect of arterial occlusion on ergogram? Rapid fatigue from ischaemia.","Normal diastolic rise in the sustained handgrip test? More than 15 mmHg."]}},"pr|Amphibian / simulated experiments":{"d":"## Principle and context\n**Frog** (*Rana tigrina / Hoplobatrachus*) nerve–muscle and heart preparations were the classical teaching tools for:\n- excitability\n- the properties of skeletal and cardiac muscle\n- autonomic control\n\nFrog tissues work at **room temperature** without oxygenated perfusion for hours, with **Ringer solution** (Sydney Ringer, 1882).\n\nSince the **Wildlife (Protection) Act** and **MCI/UGC directions (2011–2012)** restricting animal dissection in UG teaching, Indian medical colleges use:\n- **computer-assisted learning (CAL)**\n- **simulations** (e.g. Ex Pharm, PhysioEx, SimMuscle, virtual frog labs)\n- recorded **demonstration videos**\n\nThese are mandated under **CBME (2019 onwards)**. The detailed individual experiments are in the Amphibian experiments section.\n\n## Apparatus (traditional)\n- **Kymograph**: a smoked-drum or electrically driven recording drum (speeds variable).\n- **Inductorium / electronic stimulator**: single and repeated stimuli; \"make\" and \"break\" shocks (break is stronger).\n- **Simple key, short-circuit key**, electrodes.\n- **Muscle trough / moist chamber** with lever (**simple lever** or **after-loaded lever**), **Starling's heart lever**.\n- **Tuning fork / time marker** (e.g. **100 Hz**, so each wave = 0.01 s) for timing.\n- **Frog Ringer**:\n  - NaCl ≈ 0.65%\n  - KCl 0.014%\n  - CaCl₂ 0.012%\n  - NaHCO₃ 0.02%\n  - glucose\n  - (a range is given because formulations vary)\n  - **Isotonic for amphibians ≈ 0.65% NaCl vs 0.9% for mammals**\n\n## Typical experiments and key results\n### Simple muscle curve (gastrocnemius–sciatic)\n- **Latent period ≈ 0.01 s**\n- **Contraction ≈ 0.04 s**\n- **Relaxation ≈ 0.05 s**\n- **Total ≈ 0.1 s**\n\n### Other skeletal muscle experiments\n- **Effect of temperature**: warmth shortens all phases and raises height; cold prolongs.\n- **Effect of load**: free-loaded vs after-loaded. Work is maximal at an intermediate load.\n- **Two successive stimuli**: summation (wave summation).\n- **Genesis of tetanus**: incomplete then complete tetanus with increasing frequency.\n- **Fatigue**: NMJ is the first site in the preparation.\n- **Nerve conduction velocity** in frog sciatic ≈ **30 m/s** (sources 7–40 m/s depending on temperature and fibres).\n\n### Frog heart\n- **Normal cardiogram**.\n- **All-or-none law** and **staircase (Treppe)**.\n- **Long refractory period**: no tetanus.\n- **Extrasystole and compensatory pause**.\n- **Stannius ligatures**: pacemaker hierarchy, sinus venosus (the frog's pacemaker).\n- **Vagal stimulation and vagal escape**.\n- **Effects of ions** (Ca²⁺ rigor, K⁺ diastolic arrest) and **drugs** (acetylcholine, adrenaline, atropine).\n\n## Simulation procedure (CBME)\n1. Students choose parameters (stimulus strength, frequency, load, temperature).\n2. They record the virtual trace and measure amplitude and periods.\n3. They plot graphs, e.g. strength–response showing threshold, submaximal, maximal and **recruitment** in a nerve–muscle preparation.\n4. They answer reflection questions.\n\n**Advantages**: ethical (**3 Rs: Replacement, Reduction, Refinement**), repeatable, no tissue failure, self-paced.\n**Limitations**: no hands-on skill, idealised data.\n\n## Sources of error (wet lab)\n- Drying of the preparation (keep moist with Ringer).\n- Fatigue from too-frequent stimulation.\n- Polarisation of electrodes.\n- Friction of the writing lever on the drum.\n- Wrong drum speed.\n- Temperature fluctuations.\n- Nerve damage from handling with metal instruments.\n- Simulations: incorrect parameter entry; misreading scales.","q":{"b":["Frog Ringer ≈ 0.65% NaCl (isotonic for amphibians).","Simple twitch: LP 0.01, contraction 0.04, relaxation 0.05 s.","Time marker 100 Hz = 0.01 s per wave.","Break shock stronger than make shock.","Warmth shortens and heightens the twitch.","Frog nerve conduction ≈ 30 m/s (range 7–40).","Heart: long refractory period → cannot be tetanised.","Sinus venosus = frog pacemaker.","Animal dissection restricted in India since 2011–12.","CBME uses CAL/simulations – 3 Rs.","Fatigue first at NMJ in the nerve–muscle preparation."],"n":["Twitch 0.1 s","Ringer NaCl 0.65%","Time marker 100 Hz","Frog NCV ≈ 30 m/s"],"m":"**3 Rs** of animal ethics – **Replace, Reduce, Refine**.","v":["Why can't cardiac muscle be tetanised? Long refractory period, lasting through most of the contraction.","Pacemaker of the frog heart? Sinus venosus.","Duration of the latent period in a frog simple twitch? About 0.01 s."]}},"pr|Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR)":{"d":"## Principle\n**PEFR** is the **maximum flow rate** during a **forced expiration** starting from **full inspiration (TLC)**.\n\n- It occurs within the **first ≈ 100–120 ms** of expiration.\n- It is **effort-dependent**, unlike the later, effort-independent part of the flow–volume curve.\n- It mainly reflects the calibre of **large airways** and expiratory muscle strength.\n\nIt is measured with the **Wright peak flow meter** or the cheap portable **mini-Wright meter**: a spring-loaded piston or vane displaced by expired air moves a pointer along a scale in **L/min**.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Explain and demonstrate. The subject **stands** (or sits upright). Set the pointer to zero; hold the meter horizontal without obstructing the scale or vent.\n2. **Inhale maximally**, seal the lips around the mouthpiece (tongue not obstructing), and **blow out as hard and fast as possible** in a short sharp \"huff\". A complete expiration is not needed.\n3. Record the reading. Repeat **3 times** with rest; take the **highest** value. The best two should agree within about **20 L/min** (or 10%).\n4. Compare with **predicted values** for age, sex and height (nomograms; Indian equations give lower predictions than Caucasian ones).\n\n## Normal values\n- **Adult males ≈ 450–650 L/min** (≈ **8–10 L/s**).\n- **Adult females ≈ 320–470 L/min**.\n- Values peak in the 20s–30s, then decline with age.\n- Indian studies often report ≈ 400–550 L/min in young males (a range because studies vary).\n- **Diurnal variation**: lowest in the **early morning**. Normal variability **< 10–20%**.\n\n## Clinical uses\n- **Asthma**:\n  - diagnosis: **diurnal variability > 10%** (GINA, average over 1–2 weeks), or > 20% with twice-daily readings\n  - home monitoring and action plans:\n    - **green zone > 80% personal best**\n    - **yellow 50–80%**\n    - **red < 50%**\n  - severity of acute asthma: **PEF 33–50% predicted = acute severe**; **< 33% = life-threatening** (BTS)\n- **Occupational asthma**: serial measurements at work vs off work.\n- **COPD**: PEF underestimates severity, so **spirometry (FEV₁/FVC) is preferred for diagnosis**.\n- Low cost, so useful in primary care across India.\n\n## Factors affecting PEFR\n- **Age, sex, height** (most important).\n- Posture: lower when supine.\n- Effort and technique.\n- Smoking, air pollution and biomass smoke (relevant in India).\n- Training: higher in athletes and wind instrumentalists.\n- Upper-airway obstruction markedly reduces PEFR relative to FEV₁ (**Empey index = FEV₁ (ml) / PEFR (L/min) > 10** suggests upper-airway obstruction).\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Submaximal inspiration** or effort.\n- **Tongue or teeth blocking** the mouthpiece.\n- **\"Spitting\" or coughing**, giving falsely high readings.\n- Pointer not reset.\n- Finger obstructing the scale.\n- Leak at the lips.\n- Meter wear: old mini-Wright meters read inaccurately, so the **EU scale (EN 13826)** replaced the Wright scale in 2004.\n- Different meters are not interchangeable: use the same meter for monitoring.","q":{"b":["PEFR = max flow in forced expiration from TLC.","Reached within first ~0.1 s; effort-dependent.","Reflects large airways.","Wright / mini-Wright meter; units L/min.","Best of 3; agree within 20 L/min.","Men ≈ 450–650, women ≈ 320–470 L/min.","Lowest early morning.","Diurnal variability > 10% (GINA) supports asthma.","Zones: green > 80%, yellow 50–80%, red < 50% of best.","Acute severe 33–50%; life-threatening < 33%.","Empey index > 10 → upper-airway obstruction.","Spirometry preferred for COPD diagnosis."],"n":["Men 450–650 L/min","Women 320–470 L/min","Variability > 10%","3 attempts, best taken"],"m":"Asthma zones = **traffic lights**: Green > 80, Yellow 50–80, Red < 50% of personal best.","v":["Is PEFR effort-dependent? Yes, highly.","When is PEFR lowest in the day? Early morning.","PEF < 33% predicted in acute asthma means? Life-threatening asthma."]}},"pr|Artificial respiration & Basic Life Support (CPR)":{"d":"## Principle\nWhen breathing and/or circulation stop, **irreversible brain damage begins within ≈ 4–6 minutes** of anoxia.\n\n**Basic Life Support (BLS)** maintains:\n- oxygen delivery, by **rescue breaths / ventilation**\n- circulation, by **chest compressions**\n\nuntil **defibrillation** and advanced care are available. Compressions generate flow by **direct cardiac compression** and the **thoracic pump** mechanism. Good CPR gives ≈ **25–30% of normal cardiac output**.\n\nClassic manual methods (**Schafer's prone pressure**, **Silvester's**, **Holger–Nielsen**) are historical. Today **mouth-to-mouth / bag–mask ventilation** and **compression-first CPR (C-A-B)** are standard (AHA 2020; ILCOR 2025 updates retain these core numbers).\n\n## Adult BLS sequence (lay / healthcare)\n1. **Scene safety**.\n2. **Check responsiveness** (tap and shout).\n3. **Shout for help; activate the emergency response** (**108 / 112 in India**) and **get an AED**.\n4. **Check breathing and pulse** (carotid) **for no more than 10 s**. **Agonal gasps are not normal breathing**.\n5. **Start CPR: 30 compressions : 2 breaths**.\n   - **Position**: centre of the chest, **lower half of the sternum**, heel of the hand, the other hand on top, arms straight, shoulders over the hands. The patient lies on a firm surface.\n   - **Rate 100–120/min**.\n   - **Depth 5–6 cm** (2–2.4 in) in adults.\n   - **Allow full chest recoil**. **Minimise interruptions**: chest compression fraction **> 60% (ideally ≥ 80%)**.\n   - **Breaths**: open the airway by **head-tilt, chin-lift** (**jaw thrust** if spinal injury is suspected). Each breath **over ≈ 1 s**, enough to make the **chest visibly rise** (≈ 500–600 ml). Avoid excessive ventilation.\n   - **Hands-only CPR** is acceptable for untrained lay rescuers.\n6. **AED**: switch on, attach the pads, follow the prompts. Shock if **VF or pulseless VT**, then **resume CPR immediately for 2 min**.\n7. **Switch compressors every 2 min** (fatigue).\n8. With an **advanced airway**: continuous compressions plus **1 breath every 6 s (10/min)**.\n9. Respiratory arrest with a pulse: **1 breath every 6 s**.\n\n## Children and infants\n- **Single rescuer 30:2**; **two rescuers 15:2**.\n- **Depth**: about **one-third of the AP chest diameter**:\n  - **≈ 5 cm in children**\n  - **≈ 4 cm in infants**\n- **Infants**: **two fingers** (single rescuer) or the **two-thumb encircling** technique.\n- Pulse check: brachial in infants.\n- **Newborn resuscitation (NRP / NSSK in India)**: **3:1** compressions to breaths.\n\n## Recovery position and choking\n- **Recovery position** (lateral) for an unresponsive patient who is breathing.\n- **Choking**:\n  - adult: **abdominal thrusts (Heimlich)**\n  - infant: **5 back blows + 5 chest thrusts**\n\n## Physiology points\n- **Coronary perfusion pressure** (aortic diastolic minus RA pressure) **> 15–20 mmHg** predicts ROSC.\n- **ETCO₂ < 10 mmHg** after 20 min of CPR suggests poor quality or prognosis.\n- **Survival falls ≈ 7–10% for each minute** without CPR or defibrillation.\n- Expired air contains **≈ 16–17% O₂**, which is enough for rescue breaths.\n\n## Sources of error (common mistakes)\n- **Too slow or too shallow** compressions; leaning (incomplete recoil).\n- Long pauses (pulse checks, airway attempts).\n- **Hyperventilation**: raises intrathoracic pressure, reduces venous return, causes gastric inflation.\n- Wrong hand position (rib fractures, liver injury).\n- Delayed AED.\n- Soft mattress (use a backboard).\n- Not checking scene safety.\n- Delay in calling for help.","q":{"b":["Brain damage begins after ~4–6 min of anoxia.","Sequence C-A-B (compressions first).","Check pulse/breathing ≤ 10 s; agonal gasps ≠ breathing.","Adult 30:2; rate 100–120/min; depth 5–6 cm.","Full recoil; minimise interruptions (CCF > 60–80%).","Breath over 1 s; visible chest rise; avoid hyperventilation.","AED early: shock VF/pulseless VT then 2 min CPR.","Swap compressors every 2 min.","Advanced airway: 1 breath every 6 s.","Child 2 rescuers 15:2; depth 1/3 AP (5 cm child, 4 cm infant).","Neonate 3:1.","India emergency number 108/112.","Survival ↓ 7–10% per minute without CPR."],"n":["Rate 100–120/min","Depth 5–6 cm","30:2 adult","Pulse check ≤ 10 s","1 breath / 6 s with airway"],"m":"**\"Push Hard, Push Fast, Let it Recoil\"**; numbers **30-2-100-120-5-6**.","v":["Compression rate in adult CPR? 100–120 per minute.","Compression:ventilation ratio for two-rescuer child CPR? 15:2.","Shockable rhythms? Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia."]}},"pr|General examination, history-taking & hand hygiene":{"d":"## Principle\nClinical examination starts with a **structured history**. About **70–80% of diagnoses** can be suspected from the history alone (a commonly quoted, variable figure). This is followed by a **systematic general physical examination** before the systems.\n\nProfessional conduct is assessed in CBME AETCOM:\n- **introduce yourself**\n- take **informed consent**\n- respect privacy\n- use a **chaperone** for intimate examinations\n- **communicate in the patient's language**\n\n## History-taking format\n1. **Identification**: name, age, sex, occupation, address (region suggests endemic disease, e.g. fluorosis, malaria, kala-azar), socio-economic status (**modified Kuppuswamy scale**).\n2. **Chief complaints** in chronological order with duration, in the patient's own words.\n3. **History of present illness**: onset, duration, progression. For pain, use **SOCRATES**: Site, Onset, Character, Radiation, Associations, Time course, Exacerbating/relieving, Severity.\n4. **Past history**: TB, diabetes, hypertension, asthma, epilepsy, surgery, transfusions.\n5. **Drug and allergy history**.\n6. **Personal history**: diet (veg/non-veg), appetite, sleep, bowel and bladder, **addictions** (tobacco, alcohol, gutkha), sexual history.\n7. **Family history**: consanguinity (common in South India), hereditary disease.\n8. **Menstrual and obstetric history** in women.\n9. **Immunisation history** in children.\n10. Treatment history.\n\n## General physical examination\n- **Consciousness**, orientation, **built and nourishment**. **BMI** = weight (kg)/height² (m²). **Asian/Indian cut-offs**:\n  - **normal 18.5–22.9**\n  - **overweight ≥ 23**\n  - **obese ≥ 25** (WHO Asia-Pacific, adopted in India)\n- **Waist circumference**: abdominal obesity at **≥ 90 cm in men, ≥ 80 cm in women** (Indian guidelines).\n- Ordered signs, often taught as **\"PICCLE\"**:\n  - **Pallor**: lower palpebral conjunctiva, tongue, palms, nail bed.\n  - **Icterus**: sclera, in natural light. Visible when **bilirubin > 2–3 mg/dL**.\n  - **Cyanosis**:\n    - **central** (tongue, lips) when **reduced Hb > 5 g/dL** in capillary blood (Lundsgaard; newer texts state ≈ 3 g/dL in arterial blood)\n    - **peripheral** (nail beds) when the periphery is cold\n  - **Clubbing**: loss of the nail-bed angle (> 180°, **Lovibond's angle**); **Schamroth window test**.\n  - **Lymphadenopathy**: cervical, axillary, inguinal and other groups.\n  - **Edema**: **pitting**, pressed for 10–15 s over a bony surface (shin, sacrum in bed-bound patients).\n- Also look at the thyroid, skin, hair, nails (koilonychia in iron deficiency) and gait.\n- **Vital signs**:\n  - temperature: oral normal **≈ 36.5–37.2 °C (97.7–99 °F)**; axillary ≈ 0.5 °C lower\n  - **pulse 60–100/min**\n  - **RR 12–20/min**\n  - **BP < 120/80 optimal**\n  - SpO₂ ≥ 95%\n  - pain score\n\n## Hand hygiene (WHO)\n### Five moments\n1. Before touching a patient.\n2. Before a clean or aseptic procedure.\n3. After body-fluid exposure risk.\n4. After touching a patient.\n5. After touching the patient's surroundings.\n\n### Technique\n- **Alcohol-based hand rub: 20–30 s**.\n- **Soap-and-water wash: 40–60 s**.\n- Use soap and water when hands are visibly soiled, or for *C. difficile* / norovirus.\n\n### WHO steps\n1. Palm to palm.\n2. Right palm over the left dorsum with interlaced fingers, and vice versa.\n3. Palm to palm with fingers interlaced.\n4. Backs of the fingers to opposing palms.\n5. Rotational rubbing of the thumbs.\n6. Fingertips in the palm.\n\nRemove rings and watches; keep nails short. **Bare below the elbows**.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Leading questions; jargon; no interpreter.\n- Examining jaundice under yellow or artificial light.\n- Pallor in dark skin missed: use the conjunctiva.\n- Room too cold, causing peripheral cyanosis.\n- Wrong cuff size.\n- Oral temperature after hot drinks.\n- Incomplete hand-rub coverage: the **thumbs and fingertips** are most often missed.","q":{"b":["History often gives most of the diagnosis.","Pain history – SOCRATES.","Kuppuswamy scale for SES in India.","GPE: PICCLE – Pallor, Icterus, Cyanosis, Clubbing, Lymphadenopathy, Edema.","Icterus visible > 2–3 mg/dL bilirubin.","Central cyanosis: reduced Hb > 5 g/dL (capillary).","Clubbing: Lovibond angle > 180°; Schamroth window lost.","Indian BMI: overweight ≥ 23, obese ≥ 25.","Waist ≥ 90 cm men / ≥ 80 cm women.","Temp 36.5–37.2 °C; axillary ~0.5 °C lower.","WHO 5 moments of hand hygiene.","Rub 20–30 s; wash 40–60 s.","Thumbs and fingertips most missed."],"n":["BMI 18.5–22.9 (Asian)","Bilirubin > 2–3 mg/dL","Reduced Hb > 5 g/dL","Hand rub 20–30 s","Hand wash 40–60 s"],"m":"General examination **PICCLE** – Pallor, Icterus, Cyanosis, Clubbing, Lymphadenopathy, Edema.","v":["Duration of an alcohol hand rub? 20–30 seconds.","Level of reduced Hb causing central cyanosis? About 5 g/dL (capillary).","BMI cut-off for obesity in Indians? 25 kg/m² or more."]}},"pr|Clinical examination of cranial nerves":{"d":"## Principle\nThe twelve cranial nerves are tested in order, comparing **right and left**, to localise lesions in the **brainstem, base of skull, orbit and periphery**. Nuclei:\n- **III, IV**: midbrain\n- **V–VIII**: pons (V spans brainstem and cord)\n- **IX–XII**: medulla\n\n## I: Olfactory\n- Test each nostril separately, eyes closed, with **non-irritant** familiar smells (coffee, clove, peppermint, soap).\n- **Not ammonia**: it stimulates **trigeminal** pain fibres.\n- **Anosmia** in head injury (cribriform plate), frontal meningioma, Kallmann syndrome, COVID-19.\n\n## II: Optic\n- **Acuity** (Snellen), **fields** (confrontation), **colour** (Ishihara), **fundoscopy** (papilloedema, optic atrophy).\n- **Pupillary light reflex**:\n  - **afferent II, efferent III** (parasympathetic, via the Edinger–Westphal nucleus)\n  - **direct and consensual** responses\n- **Relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn)** on the **swinging-flashlight test**.\n\n## III, IV, VI: eye movements\n- Test **in an H-pattern**, looking for diplopia and nystagmus. **Accommodation reflex**: convergence, constriction, lens thickening.\n- **Lateral rectus = VI**; **superior oblique = IV**; the rest are III (\"**LR6 SO4, rest 3**\").\n- **III palsy**:\n  - **ptosis**\n  - eye **\"down and out\"**\n  - **dilated pupil** in compressive lesions such as a **posterior communicating artery aneurysm**\n  - pupil spared in diabetic (microvascular) palsy\n- **IV palsy**: vertical diplopia on looking down (stairs); head tilt away.\n- **VI palsy**: failure of abduction. A **false localising sign** in raised intracranial pressure.\n- **Argyll Robertson pupil**: accommodates but does not react to light (neurosyphilis, diabetes).\n\n## V: Trigeminal\n- **Sensory**: touch and pain over **V1 (ophthalmic), V2 (maxillary) and V3 (mandibular)**. The angle of the jaw is **C2**, not V.\n- **Motor**: masseter and temporalis (clench), **pterygoids**. On opening, the jaw deviates **to the side of the lesion**.\n- **Corneal reflex**:\n  - afferent **V1**, efferent **VII**\n  - touch the cornea (not the conjunctiva) with a wisp of cotton from the side\n- **Jaw jerk**.\n\n## VII: Facial\n- Motor: raise the eyebrows, close the eyes tight, show the teeth, puff the cheeks.\n- **UMN lesion (stroke)**: lower face only; **forehead spared**, because the upper face has **bilateral cortical supply**.\n- **LMN lesion (Bell's palsy)**: whole half of the face; **Bell's phenomenon** (the eye rolls up on attempted closure).\n- Also: **taste of the anterior two-thirds** of the tongue (chorda tympani), hyperacusis (nerve to stapedius), lacrimation.\n\n## VIII: Vestibulocochlear\n- **Hearing**: whisper test, Rinne, Weber (see Hearing tests).\n- **Vestibular**: nystagmus, **Romberg**, gait, head-impulse test, Dix–Hallpike (BPPV), caloric test (COWS: Cold Opposite, Warm Same).\n\n## IX, X: Glossopharyngeal, Vagus\n- Say \"aah\": the **uvula deviates to the normal side** (away from the lesion in X palsy).\n- **Gag reflex**: afferent IX, efferent X.\n- Voice (hoarseness in recurrent laryngeal palsy), swallowing, cough.\n- Taste of the **posterior one-third** of the tongue (IX).\n\n## XI: Accessory\n- **Sternocleidomastoid**: turn the head against resistance; weakness on turning **to the opposite side**.\n- **Trapezius**: shrug the shoulders.\n\n## XII: Hypoglossal\n- Inspect the tongue in the mouth for **wasting and fasciculation** (LMN).\n- On protrusion, the tongue **deviates towards the side of the lesion** (unopposed genioglossus).\n\n## Clinical patterns\n- **Bulbar palsy (LMN)**: wasted, fasciculating tongue; absent jaw jerk.\n- **Pseudobulbar palsy (bilateral UMN)**: small spastic tongue, **brisk jaw jerk**, emotional lability.\n- **Cerebellopontine angle tumour (acoustic schwannoma)**: V, VII, VIII.\n- **Jugular foramen syndrome**: IX, X, XI.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Ammonia for smell.\n- Testing the corneal reflex from the front (a visual blink, not the reflex).\n- Not comparing the two sides.\n- Bright ambient light during the pupil test.\n- Patient's dentures or a dry tongue.\n- Inspecting the tongue only when protruded (fasciculations are seen best at rest in the floor of the mouth).\n- Pre-existing squint confusing ocular palsy.","q":{"b":["Nuclei: III–IV midbrain, V–VIII pons, IX–XII medulla.","No ammonia for smell – stimulates V.","Light reflex: afferent II, efferent III.","LR6 SO4, rest III.","III palsy: ptosis, down-and-out, dilated pupil (PComm aneurysm).","VI palsy = false localising sign of raised ICP.","Corneal reflex: afferent V1, efferent VII.","Jaw deviates to lesion side (V motor).","UMN facial palsy spares forehead; LMN (Bell's) doesn't.","Gag reflex: IX afferent, X efferent.","Uvula deviates away from the lesion.","Tongue deviates towards the lesion (XII).","CP angle: V, VII, VIII."],"n":["12 nerves","Taste ant 2/3 – VII; post 1/3 – IX","Angle of jaw – C2"],"m":"Eye muscles **LR6 SO4 R3**; caloric **COWS** – Cold Opposite, Warm Same.","v":["Why is the forehead spared in UMN facial palsy? The upper face receives bilateral cortical innervation.","Afferent and efferent limbs of the corneal reflex? V1 (ophthalmic) and VII (facial).","Direction of tongue deviation in hypoglossal palsy? Towards the side of the lesion."]}}});